{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import pathlib; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind\\\"); names=[\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\",\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/expected.json\\\"]; [(print(\\\"FILE\\\",n),print((p/n).read_text())) for n in names]; s=(p/\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md\\\").read_text().splitlines(); print(\\\"BRIEF MATCHES\\\"); [print(i+1,x) for i,x in enumerate(s) if \\\"due day\\\" in x.lower() or x.startswith(\\\"| D \\\")]; s=(p/\\\"docs/DECISIONS-log.md\\\").read_text().splitlines(); exec(\\\"for d in [\\\\\\\"D414\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"D210\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n start=next(i for i,x in enumerate(s) if x.startswith(\\\\\\\"## \\\\\\\"+d+\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"))\\\\n end=next((i for i in range(start+1,len(s)) if s[i].startswith(\\\\\\\"## D\\\\\\\")),len(s))\\\\n print(\\\\\\\"DECISION\\\\\\\",d); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s[start:end]))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import pathlib; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind\\\"); names=[\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\",\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/expected.json\\\"]; [(print(\\\"FILE\\\",n),print((p/n).read_text())) for n in names]; s=(p/\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md\\\").read_text().splitlines(); print(\\\"BRIEF MATCHES\\\"); [print(i+1,x) for i,x in enumerate(s) if \\\"due day\\\" in x.lower() or x.startswith(\\\"| D \\\")]; s=(p/\\\"docs/DECISIONS-log.md\\\").read_text().splitlines(); exec(\\\"for d in [\\\\\\\"D414\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"D210\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n start=next(i for i,x in enumerate(s) if x.startswith(\\\\\\\"## \\\\\\\"+d+\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"))\\\\n end=next((i for i in range(start+1,len(s)) if s[i].startswith(\\\\\\\"## D\\\\\\\")),len(s))\\\\n print(\\\\\\\"DECISION\\\\\\\",d); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s[start:end]))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"FILE docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\n[\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   15\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The topic treats the commitments as already underway, so the check is due at the next annual report around February 2027, not April 1.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support business model?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of credit support / revenue-share-backed capacity NVIDIA has committed to AI cloud operators under the announced model, as disclosed in its filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the model is presented as already being introduced/underway.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K) and interim 10-Qs/8-Ks disclosing guarantees, credit support and revenue-share commitments to AI cloud partners.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\",\n    \"text\": \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-01 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"Total dollar amount of credit support / revenue-share-backed capacity NVIDIA has committed to AI cloud operators under the announced model, as disclosed in its filings.\",\n    \"question\": \"What total amount in USD of credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity has NVIDIA committed to AI cloud operators under its announced model, as disclosed in its FY2027 annual report?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 1,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 1,000,000,000 USD to below 3,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 3,000,000,000 USD to below 7,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 7,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"15,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"The topic and item do not establish that larger credit-support or revenue-share-backed commitments are better for NVIDIA, while such commitments represent support or capacity it has committed.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-carried-run-2026-11-16/stage-a\",\n   3\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The next annual report is expected around February 2027, not April 1.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n    \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K (fiscal 2027), specifically its purchase obligations / contractual commitments disclosure and related notes.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n    \"title\": \"Top Analyst Reports for NVIDIA, Berkshire Hathaway & GE Aerospace\",\n    \"text\": \"Tuesday, September 22, 2026\\nThe Zacks Research Daily presents the best research output of our analyst team. Today's Research Daily features new research reports on 16 major stocks, including NVIDIA Corp. (NVDA), Berkshire Hathaway Inc. (BRK.B) and GE Aerospace (GE). These research reports have been hand-picked from the roughly 70 reports published by our analyst team today.\\nYou can see all of today's research reports here >>>\\nThe daily 'Ahead of Wall Street' article is a must-read for all investors who would like to be ready for that day's trading action. The article comes out before the market opens, attempting to make sense of that morning's economic releases and how they will affect that day's market action. You can read this article for free on our home page and can actually sign up there to get an email notification as this article comes out each morning.\\nYou can read today's AWS here >>> Still Waters Bring Mixed Pre-Market Levels\\nShares of NVIDIA have gained +27.8% over the past year against the Zacks Semiconductor - General industry's gain of +35.6%. The company continues to benefit from broad demand for accelerated computing as customers build AI factories across hyperscalers, AI clouds, enterprises and sovereign buyers.\\nBlackwell Ultra is supporting growth while Vera Rubin broadens the platform across GPUs, CPUs, networking and software. Customer diversification, rising system content and usage-linked opportunities extend the runway, while cash generation supports capital returns. China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\nCustomer concentration, rising operating investment and competition add uncertainty. Even so, platform leadership, product cadence and expanding AI infrastructure demand give the positive drivers greater weight in the current risk-reward profile.\\n(You can read the full research report on NVIDIA here >>> )\\nBerkshire Hathaway's shares have gained +1.6% over the past year against the Zacks Insurance - Property and Casualty industry's gain of +2.8%. The company benefits from diversified businesses, disciplined underwriting and a large liquidity base that support durable earnings capacity and flexible capital allocation. Insurance float remains a low-cost funding source, while manufacturing, services, BNSF and Berkshire Hathaway Energy broaden the earnings mix.\\nRecent acquisitions and renewed share repurchases show multiple avenues for deploying capital. Still, insurance results remain exposed to catastrophe losses and rising claim frequency and severity. Lower short-term rates are reducing insurance investment income, while BNSF and BHE require substantial capital spending.\\nGreg Abel is now leading the company, leaving execution under the new leadership structure as an important watchpoint. These offsets support an overall balanced risk-reward profile and a Neutral view.\\n(You can read the full research report on Berkshire Hathaway here >>> )\\nShares of GE Aerospace have outperformed the Zacks Aerospace - Defense industry over the past year (+4.9% vs. -13.2%). The company has been witnessing strength in its businesses, driven by robust demand for commercial engines, propulsion and additive technologies. Rising U.S. & international defense budgets, geopolitical tensions, positive airline & airframer dynamics and robust demand for commercial air travel augur well for the company.\\nGE Aerospace's portfolio-reshaping actions are likely to unlock value for its shareholders. The company raised its dividend by 30.6% to 36 cents per share in February 2026.\\nHowever, it has been dealing with high costs and expenses related to certain projects and restructuring activities, which are likely to affect its margins and profitability. Supply-chain disruptions may also result in delays and increased costs. Given GE Aerospace's international presence, foreign exchange headwinds might be worrying for the company.\\n(You can read the full research report on GE Aerospace here >>> )\\nOther noteworthy reports we are featuring today include Intel Corp. (INTC), Morgan Stanley (MS) and Arm Holdings plc (ARM).\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong demand for AI and data-center infrastructure is likely to support Intel's top line. Its focus on AI-ready computing and advanced networking solutions is positive.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, rising adoption of compute platforms across cloud artificial intelligence is benefiting ARM. However, Elevated expenses due to increased investments remain a concern.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Dollar Tree is benefiting from broad category growth, multi-price adoption and store improvements, while measured expansion and reinvestment support future sales.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Textron gains from diversified exposure across business aviation, helicopters, defense platforms and aftermarket services, supported by strong demand and defense modernization.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Antero Resources' expansion of its core West Virginia Marcellus position enhances its production outlook. However, its exposure to commodity price volatility raises concerns.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Hexcel Corporation is likely to benefit from the improving air traffic. Yet labor shortage result in delays and likely impact operating results.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, SL Green benefits from strong leasing, rising rents and broad tenant demand. However, elevated debt, interest expenses and refinancing exposure remain material risks.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, solid IB business, strategic alliances/acquisitions, global footprint and Morgan Stanley's focus on less capital-markets-dependent operations will likely support its financials.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, growing 800G demand and 1.6T FiberEdge and CopperEdge ramps are driving Semtech's data center growth.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong Xifaxan growth, strategic acquisitions and new product approvals fuel Bausch Health's growth prospects. However, uncertainty around Bausch + Lomb separation is a concern.\\nThe Zacks analyst is concerned with Boston Scientific facing a sharp U.S. WATCHMAN market slowdown, as higher clinical evidence impacts referral trends. Portfolio gaps within CRM are also worrying.\\nPer the Zacks Analyst, Rigetti faces rising losses, execution and dilution risks, though cloud expansion, Novera sales, government ties and a solid cash position support longer-term potential.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, NVR faces housing affordability constraints, softer settlements and margin pressure. Also, lower home prices and higher lot costs remain concerns.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-22 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n    \"question\": \"What amount of purchase obligations and supply commitments will NVIDIA report as of FY2027 year-end in its FY2027 Form 10-K, according to its purchase-obligations and contractual-commitments disclosures and related notes?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 50,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"150,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Versus the prior year\",\n      \"why\": \"The tracked outcome is the reported FY2027 amount, not its change compared with the prior year.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"These are obligations NVIDIA has committed to pay, and the item does not establish that a higher amount is better for the business.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   90\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The next annual report is due around February 2027, not April 1.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments be at its next annual report, and could that obligation pressure its cash or credit position if AI demand disappoints?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, against the ~$119 billion the item cites as the current reference.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the supply commitments are presented as already undertaken/outstanding.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K purchase commitments / contractual obligations disclosure and interim Form 10-Q updates.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n    \"text\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"Total dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, against the ~$119 billion the item cites as the current reference.\",\n    \"question\": \"What total dollar amount of NVIDIA supply purchase commitments will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as measured in the report’s purchase commitments / contractual obligations disclosure?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 90,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 90,000,000,000 USD to below 110,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 110,000,000,000 USD to below 130,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 130,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"150,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the obligation could pressure NVIDIA’s cash or credit position if AI demand disappoints.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a conditional business-impact question, while the tracked outcome is the disclosed amount of supply purchase commitments.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"119000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"A lower amount means NVIDIA has less committed spending that could pressure cash or credit if AI demand disappoints.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   20\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The investment is already undertaken, so the check is due at the next annual report around February 2027, not April 1; the $5 billion invested is not necessarily the year-end value of the equity stake.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"For a listed holding the 10-K amount 'as of FY2027 year-end' is its market value, which moves with Intel's share price (up about 465% in a year per the article), yet the reference is the $5 billion purchase amount (another measure, not the stake's last reported value), the bands are about 2% either side of it, and a higher value, a gain for NVIDIA, is called bearish.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How large is NVIDIA's equity investment in Intel, and will the $5 billion stake be completed and held at that level as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel and whether the reported $5 billion investment is completed, as disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report and any related 8-K / Schedule 13D-G filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the item presents the $5 billion as an equity investment/commitment allocated to Intel, i.e. a sized stake tracked by its amount at that report.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, together with any 8-K or Schedule 13D/13G filings disclosing the size and closing of the Intel equity stake.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\",\n    \"text\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. While currently trading above consensus analyst targets, the valuation is supported by evolving commercial fundamentals. Technically, the stock remains in a neutral-bullish trend, with a potential move toward $159.90 if it clears $150.40 resistance.\\nTradingKey - Intel Corporation (NASDAQ: INTC ) sits at $140.05, holding firm on the ascending trendline within the 2H timeframe. The RSI indicator is currently reading 55.21, positioning it as neutral-bullish, while green candles continue to soak up sell orders in close proximity to the trendline-resistance alignment. This rally translates into a ~465% gain for the company's stock over the prior 12 months, climbing from a $18.97 low, a climb so massive that the 12-month average analyst price target of $93.93 now sits 33% behind the actual price of shares, creating an enduring wedge between where analysts have modelled the stock to end and where they find it today.\\nIntel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\\nJensen Huang Picked Intel — What the Xeon 6 Selection for DGX Rubin Means\\nNVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago.\\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Development\\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis. Google inked a multiyear deal for continued use of Intel Xeon processors in Google's workload-optimized instances, including the newest Xeon 6 processors that power C4 and N4 instances. The companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing. That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\\nThis deal stack is where the current price stands relative to the average price target of $93.93 on the stock from analyst coverage. This gap represents how fast the new Intel story is unfolding versus the rate at which sell-side analysts are updating their models: Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating noting its price target remains a mere 13.9% above current levels.\\nCurrent valuation checks (per Simply Wall St) show Intel trades at a price-to-sales multiple of 13.1x vs. a suggested fair price multiple of 15.1x given its combination of foundry + AI exposure, and scores 2 of 6 on its broader valuation checks. While INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\\nINTC Technical Setup — Trendline Defense at $140, RSI 55.21, Target $159.90\\nOn the 2H chart, INTC is holding the ascending multi-touch black trendline after a series of rejections of the descending red trendlines from the $169 plus highs and is holding above the EMAs200 at $106.07 with higher lows. RSI at 55.21 is neutral-bullish with room to rise above before becoming overbought and does not have any divergence, as buyer absorption of sell orders (seen on green candles) near the trendline-resistance confluence.\\nResistance overhead sits at $150.43 to $159.97. If INTC holds the trendline, and confirms a move above $150.40, the next price target is $159.90. If it breaks down below $126.20, the next price target is $117.07.\\nEntry: Long above $150.40 — trendline and resistance cleared\\nTarget: $159.90 — trendline bounce extension\\nStop Loss: Close below $126.20 — ascending trendline fails\\nNVIDIA selection: Xeon 6 chosen as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8; $5B equity investment\\nGoogle deal: Multiyear, locked pricing 3–5 years, custom ASIC IPU co-development\\nNext earnings: July 23, 2026\\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\\nNVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next product cycle? Here's the explanation from Lip-Bu Tan: \\\"The CPU has become the orchestration layer, the control plane of the entire AI infrastructure. In inference workloads, we're starting to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs towards 1 CPU to 1 GPU.\\\" Jensen Huang's choice of Xeon 6 is a product architecture decision, by the company that's the benchmark for defining AI infrastructure, not an investment, or a promotion, from NVIDIA: Jensen's selection of Xeon 6 is the best possible validation of Intel's CPU competitive position.\\nWhat Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance?\\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY). Non-GAAP earnings per share was $0.29 per share, compared with $0.73 (GAAP) because of foundry capital investment charges for 18A. Intel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion. \\\"AI-linked revenue is now approximately 60% of the quarterly mix, as compared with a year ago when it was under 25%,\\\" said CFO David Zinsner. The quarter was defined by unprecedented demand for silicon and Intel's determination to use the entire factory network as a means to maximize output. \\\"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\\\" said CFO Zinsner.\\nWhy Is the Average Analyst Target at $93.93 When Intel Trades at $140?\\nThat's a 33% difference, which shows how rapidly the Intel story has evolved relative to the time required for sell-side equity research analysts to update their models. Cantor Fitzgerald increased its 12-month price target to $150 on July 1 while keeping a Neutral rating, stating that the new price target was 13.9% above the current stock price. The majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A. The Simple Wall St analysis places \\\"fair value\\\" Price/Sales of 15.1 vs. 13.1, which suggests that Intel is not significantly overvalued vs. peer group P/S at current trading levels.\\nIntel is up 465% over the past year and now trades 33% above the consensus analyst 12-month price target of $93.93. That gap exists because the story, now anchored around NVIDIA's investment and product choice, the Google and Microsoft signed deals, has moved a long distance relative to the ability of sell-side research analysts to update models. Those commercial agreements are no longer a rumour.\\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\\nQ1 2026 was Data Center and AI $5.1 billion (+22%), with an average of 60% AI-linked of the mix. Technicals are a defense at $140, with the average ascending trendline, plus RSI at 55.21, neutral-bullish. Confirmation above $150.40 will send prices to a $159.90 price target. A stop at $126.20 would protect against a failure. Q2 2026 guidance to be issued at July 23 earnings is the next checkpoint, where we'll hopefully see a large component of the stack materialize in guidance.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-01 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel and whether the reported $5 billion investment is completed, as disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report and any related 8-K / Schedule 13D-G filings.\",\n    \"question\": \"What dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel will NVIDIA disclose as of FY2027 year-end (January 31, 2027), as stated in its FY2027 Form 10-K and any related 8-K or Schedule 13D/13G filings?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 4,500,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 4,500,000,000 USD to below 4,900,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 4,900,000,000 USD to below 5,100,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"$5 billion equity investment\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 5,100,000,000 USD to below 5,500,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"5,500,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the reported $5 billion investment is completed.\",\n      \"why\": \"The judged outcome is the amount of the stake disclosed at FY2027 year-end, not a separate yes-or-no completion event.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the stake is held at the $5 billion level.\",\n      \"why\": \"The judged outcome is the disclosed amount, not whether it remains at a particular level.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"$5 billion equity investment\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"5000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"A lower investment amount limits cash deployed and capital at risk, while neither the topic nor the item says that a larger stake benefits NVIDIA's business.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-second-week-2026-11-15/week-continued/stage-a\",\n   5\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The question omits the SB Energy/OpenAI affiliate leases and can be read as asking about all Nvidia guarantees; April 1 is later than the next annual report in February.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"The thesis is about the guarantee 'under the SB Energy/OpenAI affiliate infrastructure leases', but the question asks only 'What maximum potential guarantee liability does Nvidia disclose', and the judge sees 'capped at $105 billion' without being told what it caps.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much will Nvidia's maximum potential guarantee liability under the SB Energy/OpenAI affiliate infrastructure leases be at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The maximum potential amount guaranteed, any amounts paid or accrued, and the remaining term of the guarantees disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027), as fixed by the reason for an obligation already undertaken.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (Form 10-K), including the guarantees note and any disclosure of payments or accruals.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-10-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Which AI Chip Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n    \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) and Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ: AVGO ) offer two different claims on the AI computing budget. Nvidia sells a broad GPU platform supported by software and integrated systems. Broadcom helps customers build custom accelerators and supplies networking alongside a substantial software business. An investor choosing between them needs to decide which company can retain more cash as customers seek cheaper computing.\\nAt the October 5 close, their trailing cash-flow valuations were close enough that the business differences matter more than a small discount. My preference is Nvidia, provided its software advantage continues to protect margins and its infrastructure guarantees do not absorb that benefit. Broadcom becomes more compelling if custom chips gain share while financing support at both companies stays contained.\\nThe custom-chip choice extends beyond these two stocks. Our Broadcom-versus-Marvell comparison examines how much investors are paying for current cash generation versus later customer ramps .\\nNvidia's margin is the advantage custom chips must erode\\nNvidia's August 26 report showed revenue of $96.2 billion for the quarter ended July 26, up 106% year over year. Its 75% GAAP gross margin indicates that buyers were still paying well above the company's production costs. CUDA's libraries, development tools and compatibility with established applications help explain the attraction: changing hardware can also require engineering work and performance validation. That creates a cost of switching beyond the processor's purchase price.\\nThe bullish case is that customers keep valuing that flexibility as models and workloads change. Nvidia can earn from a platform usable across many buyers rather than depend on the success of one customer's chip design. The bearish case is that large customers eventually find enough predictable work to justify specialized alternatives. Custom silicon need not replace every GPU to limit Nvidia's pricing power on the workloads where substitution is economical.\\nDeployment constraints can also delay demand even when the computing opportunity remains attractive. Our analysis of Nvidia's flexible AI factory asks which operating test extra funding alone cannot solve . Power availability and useful computing output belong in the demand assessment alongside a customer's announced budget.\\nHistorical ownership gives Nvidia broader long-side participation: Insider Monkey's hedge fund database recorded 285 holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1. Fisher Asset Management held 90,935,947 shares after increasing its position about 3%. Those positions predate the August results. At the September 15 settlement, 294,225,803 shares were sold short, about 1.27% of float, giving limited support for a thesis built around crowded short positioning.\\nBroadcom's September 2 results reported $29.6 billion of quarterly revenue, up 86%, for the period ended August 2. AI semiconductor revenue rose 221% to $16.7 billion. That is the strongest operating argument for choosing Broadcom: customers pursuing custom computing are already producing substantial sales. Its latest quarter also generated $13.7 billion of free cash flow, equal to 46% of revenue.\\nCustom designs can build durable engineering relationships, while networking gives Broadcom another way to earn as clusters expand. Infrastructure software supplied $8.75 billion of quarterly revenue. It offers a separate profit pool, although license revenue means the segment's sales are not uniformly recurring subscriptions. The investment case depends on retained margins and collections as much as the announced chip opportunity.\\nConcentration makes those economics more sensitive to a few buyers. Broadcom's five largest end customers accounted for 55% of quarterly revenue. A large program can support efficient development, but a buyer with alternatives can bargain for a greater share of the savings. Our dividend analysis tests how much protection today's cash coverage provides when those customers gain negotiating power .\\nThe financing risk also deserves a place in the comparison. Broadcom disclosed a maximum potential liability of about $29 billion under a five-year customer-lease backstop upon deployment of all relevant AI racks. No amounts had been paid, and the backstop's fair value was immaterial. For scale, that contingent maximum equals nearly three-quarters of its $39.4 billion annual trailing free cash flow, although the potential obligations extend across several years. A customer default and weaker equipment resale values could make financing support costly just when demand deteriorates.\\nBroadcom's hedge-fund-holder count fell to 170 in Q2 2026 from 173 in Q1. Fisher Asset Management increased its share position about 3% to 15,131,673. The manager increased both holdings while the aggregate holder counts moved differently. These disclosures describe historical exposure; the operating and valuation comparison must supply the investment judgment.\\nAt closing prices of $239.09 for Nvidia and $362.51 for Broadcom on October 5, the stocks traded at about 45.6 and 44.0 times trailing free cash flow, respectively. Both measures use equity value divided by trailing operating cash flow less capital expenditure, through July 26 for Nvidia and August 2 for Broadcom. Nvidia's premium was only about 4%. Broadcom's rapid AI growth could justify that small valuation advantage. Its customer concentration qualifies the bargain, while contingent financing exposure requires scrutiny at both companies.\\nThe cash measure also has limits. It adds back stock compensation, includes interest and can fluctuate with working capital. It does not deduct every acquisition or strategic investment. Nvidia's capital-expenditure figure includes intangible-asset purchases but excludes equipment and intangible-asset principal payments, unlike the company's own free-cash-flow definition.\\nNvidia's latest quarterly filing also disclosed guarantees signed in August, capped at $105 billion, supporting an OpenAI affiliate's infrastructure with SB Energy. Exposure generally begins as each lease commences, with the first data centers expected to be placed in service in fiscal 2029. Payments depend on specified tenant defaults; guaranteed amounts decline over 20-year leases and cover defined portions of lease and power payments. The cap is not current debt or an expected loss. Broadcom's five-year rack backstop and Nvidia's longer infrastructure guarantees have different assets, remedies and timing; their headline maximums cannot establish which stock has the safer financing profile.\\nSupply obligations add another demand risk. Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years, some adjustable or cancelable at a cost. Broadcom disclosed $126.8 billion of unconditional purchase commitments, mainly inventory. Neither total is an immediate cash bill, but slower customer spending could leave commitments harder to absorb.\\nAn illustrative multiple contraction shows the hurdle facing both stocks. If a 45-times cash-flow valuation fell to 30 times, cash flow would need to rise 50% just to preserve the same equity value, assuming no change in share count. Over two years, that requires growth of about 22.5% annually before providing capital appreciation. This is a sensitivity, not a forecast or a fair-value estimate.\\nNvidia seems preferable at this relatively small premium because its broad software platform gives it a stronger route to retaining pricing across changing workloads. That judgment would weaken if custom chips took enough economically attractive work to reduce Nvidia's margins while Broadcom sustained superior cash-flow growth, or if Nvidia's guarantee payments consumed the cash benefit of its platform. Broadcom would then offer the better exposure, provided its own financing commitments remained manageable. For now, the decisive evidence is cash growth after the costs of winning and funding demand, with continued margin strength making Nvidia my preferred stock.\\nWhile we acknowledge the potential of NVDA and AVGO as investments, we believe certain other AI stocks offer greater upside potential and carry less downside risk. If you're looking for an extremely undervalued AI stock that also stands to benefit significantly from Trump-era tariffs and the onshoring trend, see our free report on the best short-term AI stock .\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The maximum potential amount guaranteed, any amounts paid or accrued, and the remaining term of the guarantees disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n    \"question\": \"What maximum potential guarantee liability does Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 annual report (Form 10-K)?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 80,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 80,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 110,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"capped at $105 billion\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 110,000,000,000 USD to below 130,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"130,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Any amounts paid or accrued under the guarantees\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a separate figure from the maximum potential guarantee liability tracked by the topic's outcome.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The remaining term of the guarantees\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a separate measure, not the maximum potential guarantee liability tracked by the topic's outcome.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"capped at $105 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"105000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"A lower guarantee liability limits Nvidia's potential financial exposure.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/probe-second-look\",\n   9\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The already undertaken purchase obligation is due at the next annual filing around February 2027, not April 1.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, likely filed early 2027); set by the reason — an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's annual report on Form 10-K, purchase commitments / commitments and contingencies note, for the fiscal year ending late January 2027.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n    \"text\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"question\": \"What outstanding amount of supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers does NVIDIA report as of the end of FY2027 in its Form 10-K purchase commitments / commitments and contingencies note?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 80,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 80,000,000,000 USD to below 105,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 105,000,000,000 USD to below 135,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"$119B in supply commitments\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 135,000,000,000 USD to below 160,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"160,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"$119B in supply commitments\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"119000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"A lower outstanding amount means NVIDIA has committed to pay less to suppliers.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/probe-second-look\",\n   1\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The already undertaken purchase obligation is due at the next annual filing around February 2027, not April 1.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027); the item gives no date, so the date is set by the reason — an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K, in its contractual obligations / purchase commitments disclosure.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n    \"text\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\",\n    \"question\": \"What will NVIDIA’s supply purchase commitments total in FY2027, as disclosed in its next Form 10-K contractual obligations or purchase commitments disclosure?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 95,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 95,000,000,000 USD to below 110,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 110,000,000,000 USD to below 130,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 130,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"150,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The purchase obligation is large enough to bear on NVIDIA’s cash and credit risk.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is the topic’s rationale for tracking the amount, not a separate figure or condition being judged.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"119000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"A lower amount of purchase commitments means less cash and credit risk from these obligations.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-split\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-second-week-2026-11-15/week-continued/stage-a\",\n   3\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"April 1 is later than the next annual report in February specified for the obligation.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much of Nvidia's $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments will remain outstanding or unconditional at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The total supply and capacity purchase commitments and the portion that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027), as fixed by the reason for an obligation already undertaken.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (Form 10-K), including the purchase obligations or commitments table.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-10-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Which AI Chip Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n    \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) and Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ: AVGO ) offer two different claims on the AI computing budget. Nvidia sells a broad GPU platform supported by software and integrated systems. Broadcom helps customers build custom accelerators and supplies networking alongside a substantial software business. An investor choosing between them needs to decide which company can retain more cash as customers seek cheaper computing.\\nAt the October 5 close, their trailing cash-flow valuations were close enough that the business differences matter more than a small discount. My preference is Nvidia, provided its software advantage continues to protect margins and its infrastructure guarantees do not absorb that benefit. Broadcom becomes more compelling if custom chips gain share while financing support at both companies stays contained.\\nThe custom-chip choice extends beyond these two stocks. Our Broadcom-versus-Marvell comparison examines how much investors are paying for current cash generation versus later customer ramps .\\nNvidia's margin is the advantage custom chips must erode\\nNvidia's August 26 report showed revenue of $96.2 billion for the quarter ended July 26, up 106% year over year. Its 75% GAAP gross margin indicates that buyers were still paying well above the company's production costs. CUDA's libraries, development tools and compatibility with established applications help explain the attraction: changing hardware can also require engineering work and performance validation. That creates a cost of switching beyond the processor's purchase price.\\nThe bullish case is that customers keep valuing that flexibility as models and workloads change. Nvidia can earn from a platform usable across many buyers rather than depend on the success of one customer's chip design. The bearish case is that large customers eventually find enough predictable work to justify specialized alternatives. Custom silicon need not replace every GPU to limit Nvidia's pricing power on the workloads where substitution is economical.\\nDeployment constraints can also delay demand even when the computing opportunity remains attractive. Our analysis of Nvidia's flexible AI factory asks which operating test extra funding alone cannot solve . Power availability and useful computing output belong in the demand assessment alongside a customer's announced budget.\\nHistorical ownership gives Nvidia broader long-side participation: Insider Monkey's hedge fund database recorded 285 holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1. Fisher Asset Management held 90,935,947 shares after increasing its position about 3%. Those positions predate the August results. At the September 15 settlement, 294,225,803 shares were sold short, about 1.27% of float, giving limited support for a thesis built around crowded short positioning.\\nBroadcom's September 2 results reported $29.6 billion of quarterly revenue, up 86%, for the period ended August 2. AI semiconductor revenue rose 221% to $16.7 billion. That is the strongest operating argument for choosing Broadcom: customers pursuing custom computing are already producing substantial sales. Its latest quarter also generated $13.7 billion of free cash flow, equal to 46% of revenue.\\nCustom designs can build durable engineering relationships, while networking gives Broadcom another way to earn as clusters expand. Infrastructure software supplied $8.75 billion of quarterly revenue. It offers a separate profit pool, although license revenue means the segment's sales are not uniformly recurring subscriptions. The investment case depends on retained margins and collections as much as the announced chip opportunity.\\nConcentration makes those economics more sensitive to a few buyers. Broadcom's five largest end customers accounted for 55% of quarterly revenue. A large program can support efficient development, but a buyer with alternatives can bargain for a greater share of the savings. Our dividend analysis tests how much protection today's cash coverage provides when those customers gain negotiating power .\\nThe financing risk also deserves a place in the comparison. Broadcom disclosed a maximum potential liability of about $29 billion under a five-year customer-lease backstop upon deployment of all relevant AI racks. No amounts had been paid, and the backstop's fair value was immaterial. For scale, that contingent maximum equals nearly three-quarters of its $39.4 billion annual trailing free cash flow, although the potential obligations extend across several years. A customer default and weaker equipment resale values could make financing support costly just when demand deteriorates.\\nBroadcom's hedge-fund-holder count fell to 170 in Q2 2026 from 173 in Q1. Fisher Asset Management increased its share position about 3% to 15,131,673. The manager increased both holdings while the aggregate holder counts moved differently. These disclosures describe historical exposure; the operating and valuation comparison must supply the investment judgment.\\nAt closing prices of $239.09 for Nvidia and $362.51 for Broadcom on October 5, the stocks traded at about 45.6 and 44.0 times trailing free cash flow, respectively. Both measures use equity value divided by trailing operating cash flow less capital expenditure, through July 26 for Nvidia and August 2 for Broadcom. Nvidia's premium was only about 4%. Broadcom's rapid AI growth could justify that small valuation advantage. Its customer concentration qualifies the bargain, while contingent financing exposure requires scrutiny at both companies.\\nThe cash measure also has limits. It adds back stock compensation, includes interest and can fluctuate with working capital. It does not deduct every acquisition or strategic investment. Nvidia's capital-expenditure figure includes intangible-asset purchases but excludes equipment and intangible-asset principal payments, unlike the company's own free-cash-flow definition.\\nNvidia's latest quarterly filing also disclosed guarantees signed in August, capped at $105 billion, supporting an OpenAI affiliate's infrastructure with SB Energy. Exposure generally begins as each lease commences, with the first data centers expected to be placed in service in fiscal 2029. Payments depend on specified tenant defaults; guaranteed amounts decline over 20-year leases and cover defined portions of lease and power payments. The cap is not current debt or an expected loss. Broadcom's five-year rack backstop and Nvidia's longer infrastructure guarantees have different assets, remedies and timing; their headline maximums cannot establish which stock has the safer financing profile.\\nSupply obligations add another demand risk. Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years, some adjustable or cancelable at a cost. Broadcom disclosed $126.8 billion of unconditional purchase commitments, mainly inventory. Neither total is an immediate cash bill, but slower customer spending could leave commitments harder to absorb.\\nAn illustrative multiple contraction shows the hurdle facing both stocks. If a 45-times cash-flow valuation fell to 30 times, cash flow would need to rise 50% just to preserve the same equity value, assuming no change in share count. Over two years, that requires growth of about 22.5% annually before providing capital appreciation. This is a sensitivity, not a forecast or a fair-value estimate.\\nNvidia seems preferable at this relatively small premium because its broad software platform gives it a stronger route to retaining pricing across changing workloads. That judgment would weaken if custom chips took enough economically attractive work to reduce Nvidia's margins while Broadcom sustained superior cash-flow growth, or if Nvidia's guarantee payments consumed the cash benefit of its platform. Broadcom would then offer the better exposure, provided its own financing commitments remained manageable. For now, the decisive evidence is cash growth after the costs of winning and funding demand, with continued margin strength making Nvidia my preferred stock.\\nWhile we acknowledge the potential of NVDA and AVGO as investments, we believe certain other AI stocks offer greater upside potential and carry less downside risk. If you're looking for an extremely undervalued AI stock that also stands to benefit significantly from Trump-era tariffs and the onshoring trend, see our free report on the best short-term AI stock .\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The total supply and capacity purchase commitments and the portion that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n    \"question\": \"What total amount of Nvidia’s supply and capacity purchase commitments will Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K, as of FY2027 year-end?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 200,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 200,000,000,000 USD to below 260,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 260,000,000,000 USD to below 300,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"$279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 300,000,000,000 USD to below 350,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"350,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The portion of the commitments that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a separate measure of the commitments’ flexibility, while the tracked outcome names the total amount.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"$279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"279000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"Lower commitments reduce the amount Nvidia may have difficulty absorbing if customer spending slows.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-agreed\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   29\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"What will NVIDIA's outstanding supply (purchase) commitments to its manufacturing and memory suppliers total at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The supply-commitments balance NVIDIA discloses in its next Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion reported in this item, and whether the obligation is growing faster than the revenue it supports (which would raise write-down or utilisation risk if hyperscaler orders slow).\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K, fiscal year ending late January 2027) — the next annual report for an obligation already undertaken, as section 3 fixes for such obligations.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K (fiscal 2027), which discloses purchase/supply commitments outstanding, with the 10-Q filings in between as interim checks.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\",\n    \"text\": \"Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\\nAmazon, Google, Microsoft, and Meta are all building proprietary silicon to cut costs and gain negotiating leverage over NVIDIA.\\nNVIDIA's NVLink Fusion pivot keeps its interconnect sticky even as hyperscalers shift compute to custom chips, but the strategy implicitly concedes the battle over GPUs.\\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\\nNVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18 and a high of $236.26. Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\nAbout 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\nGrowth accelerates at NVIDIA’s scale. Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%. Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material. Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\\nNeither thesis has resolved. Blackwell ramps while Trainium and TPU volumes rise in parallel. NVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\\nWatch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. Any one breaking hard could tip the call.\\nNVIDIA trades at $196 against an analyst consensus target of ~$301.62 as of this writing, implying 53% upside. Coverage skews heavily positive with 10 Strong Buy, 48 Buy, 2 Hold, 1 Sell ratings. Forward P/E sits at 22x, trailing P/E at 29x.\\nPerformance is mixed. NVDA is up 4.59% year to date and 24.06% over the trailing year, but down 12.46% over the past month. The S&P 500 delivered a smaller trailing-year gain, so NVDA outperformed with more turbulence.\\nAt $196, NVIDIA remains a buy.\\nThe numbers do not argue for selling. A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise. The numbers also do not argue for aggressively adding. Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom silicon head-on loses.\\nBuy conviction requires durable evidence that networking and software capture margin even when compute goes custom, plus a China resolution. Sell conviction requires a hyperscaler capex reset or a Trainium/TPU disclosure that reframes NVIDIA as a supplier rather than the platform. Prediction markets show 80.5% conviction NVDA touches $192 in July and only 7% for a week close above $210, a range consistent with the fundamentals.\\nOwning NVIDIA at this price is defensible. Buying it aggressively requires believing the customer base will keep writing checks it is openly trying to stop writing.\\nOmor Ibne Ehsan is a writer at 24/7 Wall St. He is a self-taught investor with a focus on growth, cyclical, and dividend equities that have strong fundamentals, value, and long-term potential. He also has an interest in high-risk, high-reward investments such as penny stocks.\\nNvidia is letting a rival chipmaker plug directly into its own racks, and the move only looks like a concession until you follow the money through every layer of the data center.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The supply-commitments balance NVIDIA discloses in its next Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion reported in this item, and whether the obligation is growing faster than the revenue it supports (which would raise write-down or utilisation risk if hyperscaler orders slow).\",\n    \"question\": \"What will NVIDIA's outstanding supply (purchase) commitments to its manufacturing and memory suppliers be as of FY2027 year-end, as disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 90,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 90,000,000,000 USD to below 110,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 110,000,000,000 USD to below 130,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"Supply commitments of $119 billion\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 130,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"150,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the obligation is growing faster than the revenue it supports.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a comparison, not the outstanding supply-commitments balance named as the tracked figure.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether that growth would raise write-down or utilisation risk if hyperscaler orders slow.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a conditional risk implication, not the tracked supply-commitments balance.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"Supply commitments of $119 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"119000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"A lower outstanding commitment reduces NVIDIA's exposure if supplier purchases are not supported by customer demand.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-agreed\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-carried-run-2026-11-16/stage-a-reread\",\n   5\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027 (guarantees, commitments and contingencies note) and any 8-K or earnings disclosure quantifying the credit support for the additional AI lab's ~2 GW of computing capacity and the minimum-revenue guarantees to cloud partners.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n    \"title\": \"Is NVIDIA Funding Its Own Stock Rally?\",\n    \"text\": \"Is NVIDIA Funding Its Own Stock Rally?\\nIf you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. NVIDIA’s revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027, so this number is easy to miss. An important thing to watch is how much future demand depends on NVIDIA backing its own customers.\\nWhy Does NVIDIA Put Its Own Money Behind Its Customers?\\nHere is how this kind of support works, as a made-up example. A chipmaker invests in a young customer. That customer then spends the money on the chipmaker’s products.\\nThe sale is real, but the chipmaker’s own money sits on both sides of it. Management said in the fiscal Q2 2027 call that some will call NVIDIA’s support circular financing.\\nManagement says a surge in AI demand is driving a global buildout of computing infrastructure. It also describes revenue-sharing deals with cloud partners.\\nManagement says these deals matter because, over time, they can expand NVIDIA’s addressable market. They can also create a recurring revenue stream tied to usage. That explains why NVIDIA does it, but not how big the support has become.\\nHow Big Has NVIDIA’s Support For Its Customers Become?\\nNVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, according to management. For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity. With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\nThose lab investments are part of a broader expansion: Business Insider reported that NVIDIA’s overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion. In summer 2025, that portfolio was roughly $7 billion. That is a 14-fold increase, so this is not a mild change.\\nThere is also a change in how quickly NVIDIA gets paid. Days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days. Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters. So some buyers with strong credit are taking longer to pay.\\nWhat Are You Paying For NVIDIA Stock Today?\\nNVIDIA stock trades at 28.1 times its past year’s earnings. The S&P 500 trades at 22.5 times. The multiple also sits in the bottom tenth of NVIDIA’s own 10-year range.\\nThe price likely assumes that growth holds up and that the backed customers keep paying . Management points to the size of its customers’ commitments. OpenAI’s existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing. OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030. Whether those purchases rest on NVIDIA’s backing is worth watching.\\nNVIDIA’s own finances are also strong. Its debt is just 0.7% of its market value. For the S&P 500 as a whole, that figure is 21%, so NVIDIA carries far less debt.\\nRoughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back. That is a notable share, and it represents a key risk factor that investors should monitor. The worry would grow if that share climbs or collection times keep stretching. It would ease if more growth comes from customers paying their own way. NVIDIA’s next update comes at its fiscal Q3 2027 earnings call on November 17.\\nLearn more about NVDA | Identify catalysts | Generate income against NVDA stock\\nTrim your NVDA position | Tax-efficient rebalancing strategies | Opt for High Quality Portfolio\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-22 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n    \"question\": \"What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 5,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"50,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The amount of this support and these guarantees versus NVIDIA's equity investments.\",\n      \"why\": \"The judged figure is the total amount of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees, not a comparison with NVIDIA's equity investments.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"A lower amount means NVIDIA has less credit support and minimum-revenue guarantee exposure to fund or honor.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-agreed\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/run-3\",\n   15\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K), expected early 2027 — set by the reason: an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 Form 10-K (and the interim 10-Q purchase-commitments note), which disclose the dollar amount of supply/purchase commitments.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n    \"text\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"question\": \"What dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 100,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 110,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 110,000,000,000 USD to below 130,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 130,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"150,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the approximately $119 billion level is growing faster than the revenue those commitments support.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a comparison with revenue, not the dollar amount of commitments tracked as the outcome.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"119000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"These are commitments to pay, and neither the topic nor the item establishes that a higher amount is better for NVIDIA's business.\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"class-agreed\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/run-3\",\n   83\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much will Nvidia have guaranteed or committed in credit support for AI infrastructure projects at its next annual report, after agreeing in August 2026 to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported amount of Nvidia's guarantees / credit support for AI infrastructure projects, including the Ohio data center project, disclosed at its next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report: Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027 (filed in early 2027); set by the rule that an obligation already undertaken is tracked at the next annual report.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, including the guarantees and commitments note.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Jensen Huang Just Sent a Signal That Could Matter More Than the September Effect\",\n    \"text\": \"Historically, September has been a difficult month for Wall Street. The S&P 500 has fallen by an average of about 1.1% during the month from 1926 through 2024. Yet Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) stock has gained ground in seven of the past 10 Septembers, with a median return of about 1.5%. Hence, while the September Effect may offer a reason for some caution around the overall stock market, it hasn't been such a negative indicator for Nvidia's stock.\\nAnd recently, CEO Jensen Huang also gave investors a potentially more important signal. He said Nvidia expects revenue to grow about 70% in its fiscal 2028, which begins Jan 31, 2027. But management says even that forecast doesn't reflect the full scope of demand for its offerings; it's constrained by the limited supply of components required to build its artificial intelligence (AI) platforms.\\nNvidia's visibility into future demand is sharpening\\nIn its fiscal 2027 second quarter (which ended July 26), Nvidia's revenue soared by 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, including $89 billion in sales from the data center segment. However, management's long-term outlook was even more impressive.\\nWall Street analysts had previous expected the company to deliver fiscal 2028 revenue of about $570 billion, translating to growth of roughly 44%. Nvidia's guidance, however, implies revenue of close to $700 billion.\\nCEO Jensen Huang said that Nvidia has never offered revenue guidance for a full year in advance before. However, this time, the company has much better visibility into memory and manufacturing capacity, as well as the land, power, and data-center infrastructure needed to deploy its products. Some of this infrastructure needs to be secured two to three years in advance.\\nSupply constraints could limit Nvidia's fiscal 2028 growth\\nCFO Colette Kress highlighted that customer forecasts currently indicate Nvidia's revenue may double next year. Huang also said Nvidia's revenue growth could be \\\"a lot higher\\\" if the company were not supply-constrained.\\nNvidia exited the fiscal second quarter with $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up sequentially from $119 billion. These commitments are primarily related to memory purchases and manufacturing capacity. Of that total, $92 billion is scheduled for the rest of fiscal 2027, $87 billion for fiscal 2028, and $88 billion for fiscal 2029.\\nNvidia's largest customers are also continuing to spend heavily on AI infrastructure. Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) Web Services plans to deploy another 2 million Nvidia GPUs in calendar years 2027 and 2028. Nvidia also expects the combined capital expenditures of the top five hyperscalers to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\\nThe demand trends look impressive, even when the company is not factoring any data center compute revenue from China into its current outlook. Hence, a meaningful recovery of its position in the Chinese market is not currently part of Nvidia's growth expectations.\\nNvidia's revenue opportunity per AI data center is expanding\\nNvidia is also expanding its revenue opportunity from each gigawatt (power capacity) of AI infrastructure. Management estimates that this opportunity has increased from roughly $18 billion with Hopper GPUs to $25 billion with Blackwell systems and $40 billion with Vera Rubin systems. These increases are partly a result of the fact that Nvidia is selling more components of the overall AI system, including CPUs, GPUs, NVLink (Nvidia's high-speed technology for connecting GPUs and other processors), and other networking products. Hence, the company benefits not only from the construction of more AI data centers, but also because it is generating more revenue per gigawatt of new capacity deployed.\\nIncreasing adoption of agentic AI could further drive demand for compute capacity. Huang said AI agents can require roughly 15 to 100 times more compute than direct human use of AI, depending on the task. These agents can also run continuously and interact with other agents. Hence, future demand for Nvidia's wares may increasingly depend on the actual use of AI applications rather than on the computing power required to train AI models.\\nNvidia's growth outlook also comes with risks\\nNvidia, however, has warned that customer demand forecasts can prove inaccurate. Its customers may also delay purchases because of constraints related to data center infrastructure or capital availability. In such a scenario, Nvidia's large commitments to its own suppliers could result in higher costs.\\nNvidia is also providing support for some large AI infrastructure projects. In August, the company agreed to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio. That campus will exclusively host Nvidia computing systems under 20-year leases to OpenAI . Huang also said AI labs for which Nvidia expects to use its balance sheet could account for roughly one-quarter of the company's business in fiscal 2028.\\nRising memory costs could also pressure profitability. Nvidia expects its gross margins to fall from 75% in the fiscal second quarter to around 71% to 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter, before improving to around 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028. Management attributed much of this pressure to sharp increases in memory prices.\\nNvidia's stock also faces near-term pressure. On Sept. 1, rising Treasury yields and oil prices pushed the Nasdaq Composite down by 1%. Besides these marketwide risks, Nvidia is also facing concerns about some of its AI financing arrangements. Its stock slipped by 1.5% during the session.\\nSeptember could still bring volatility for Nvidia investors. However, it is obvious that Nvidia now has much greater visibility into demand several years ahead. If that visibility holds, short-term weaknesses could matter far less than the scale of the growth opportunity the company is preparing for.\\nBy Manali Pradhan, CFA – Sep 5, 2026 at 4:54AM EST\\nNvidia has increased its supply and capacity commitments from $119 billion to $279 billion as it prepares for future AI infrastructure demand.\\nNvidia's revenue opportunity per gigawatt could rise from about $18 billion with Hopper GPUs to $40 billion with Vera Rubin systems.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The reported amount of Nvidia's guarantees / credit support for AI infrastructure projects, including the Ohio data center project, disclosed at its next annual report.\",\n    \"question\": \"What amount of Nvidia's guarantees and credit support for AI infrastructure projects, including the Ohio project, will be disclosed in the guarantees and commitments note of its FY2027 Form 10-K?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 50,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 150,000,000,000 USD to below 250,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"250,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Nvidia's agreement in August 2026 to provide support for the Ohio data center project.\",\n      \"why\": \"The question tracks the amount disclosed at the FY2027 annual report, not whether this earlier agreement was made.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The up-to-$105 billion figure for the Ohio project.\",\n      \"why\": \"That cap is a project-specific amount, not the total amount of Nvidia's guarantees and credit support for AI infrastructure projects disclosed in the report.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"Lower guarantees and credit support mean less potential financial exposure for Nvidia.\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"fail-control\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   101\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"An H100 rental price is not a semiconductor-cycle figure, and the price on December 31 is a point-in-time figure, not a full-year figure for the annual publication date.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The thesis says the figure is checked against Ornn's index at year-end 2026 and the question measures it on 2026-12-31, so the due day should be 2026-12-31, not code's 2027-03-31 with basis expected_publication.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will the spot rental price of an NVIDIA H100 GPU-hour keep falling through calendar 2026, dropping below the ~$3.20/hour peak reached in May 2026, as custom accelerators and easing GPU supply pressure the market price of compute?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The trajectory of H100 GPU-hour spot rental prices (Ornn compute-marketplace index) over calendar 2026 — whether the rate continues to decline from the ~$3.20/hour May 2026 peak.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 — checked against Ornn's published H100 spot rental-price index at year-end 2026.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Ornn's published spot-market H100 GPU-hour rental-price index (and comparable compute-marketplace price data) at year-end 2026.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia is a victim of the compute marketplace it created\",\n    \"text\": \"Long the leading light of the industry, Nvidia has had a bad couple of months. Bloomberg has the ugly details , but the upshot is that the company’s stock price has fallen 15% since its peak in May, even as projected revenue continues to grow. Compared with expected earnings, the company is now cheaper than the S&P average; investors are paying less per dollar of Nvidia’s projected profit than they do for the typical large American company.\\nMoney is still flooding into AI infrastructure stocks, but it’s mostly going into memory companies. Over the same period, Micron — one of the world’s largest makers of DRAM, the standard type of memory chip found in computers and servers — has nearly tripled in value, establishing memory as the new bottleneck for data centers and the hot new AI trade. The basic reason is simple: The GPU shortage that looked so alarming last year has eased off a bit. At the same time, data centers need all the memory money can buy.\\nFor anyone who appreciates Nvidia’s technological accomplishments, this can feel a bit deflating. There’s a lot of genuinely impressive technology behind Nvidia’s rise, both in developing CUDA, its widely adopted programming platform that made Nvidia GPUs the default engine for AI research, and in pushing the pace of GPU development to a speed few thought possible. Nvidia’s success is the kind of thing you can write whole books about, and the GPUs themselves are among the most complex devices ever produced, right at the bleeding edge of human capability.\\nFor memory companies like Micron, the story is much simpler. They build high-bandwidth memory chips — specialized components designed to move data in and out of processors as fast as possible — which have been getting incrementally better for 20 years. Without the chips or the companies changing too much, the service they provide suddenly became very valuable — and since demand is growing faster than anyone can scale up supply, they have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year.\\nThis, via Datatrack, is what the spot price for DRAM — the price buyers pay for chips on the open market, as opposed to long-term contract rates — looks like since 2023:\\nYou might think there was some amazing technical breakthrough in the summer of 2025, but no, the industry as a whole just vastly underestimated how much memory it would need for the data center buildout.\\nIn comparison, this (via the compute market Ornn ) is how the spot price for an hour of time on an Nvidia H100 GPU has changed over the last year:\\nJust like Nvidia’s stock price, there’s a peak in May (around $3.20 an hour) and then a steady drop-off. For better or worse, Nvidia’s value as a company is tied to the price of compute and that price is falling. Micron and its cohort are tied to the price of DRAM, and that price keeps rising.\\nWhen I talked to Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms about the forces driving that disparity, he framed it as a simple issue of supply and demand. Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\\n“More GPU and accelerator players are entering the market. Everyone wants to make their own silicon, but no one is making their own DRAM,” Nelms told me. “Until there’s a major technological breakthrough on HBM [high-bandwidth memory], a shift in supply and demand, or someone new [enters the market in memory], I think things will more or less persist as we see today.”\\nIt’s a frustrating state of affairs for Nvidia, and largely a product of its own success. Having proven how valuable compute can be, the company finds itself at the center of a market everyone wants to be in — while simpler technologies and less interesting companies get rich on the sidelines.\\nRussell Brandom has been covering the tech industry since 2012, with a focus on platform policy and emerging technologies. He previously worked at The Verge and Rest of World, and has written for Wired, The Awl and MIT’s Technology Review.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-09 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"semiconductor-cycle\",\n    \"kind\": \"theme\",\n    \"focus\": \"The trajectory of H100 GPU-hour spot rental prices (Ornn compute-marketplace index) over calendar 2026 — whether the rate continues to decline from the ~$3.20/hour May 2026 peak.\",\n    \"question\": \"What is Ornn's published H100 GPU-hour spot rental price, in USD per hour, measured on December 31, 2026?\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"below 1 USD\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 1 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 1 USD to below 2 USD\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 1 USD to below 2 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 2 USD to below 3 USD\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 2 USD to below 3 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 3 USD to below 4 USD\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 3 USD to below 4 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"4 USD or more\",\n      \"condition\": \"4 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the price keeps falling through calendar 2026 and drops below the approximately $3.20/hour peak reached in May 2026\",\n      \"why\": \"The judged figure is the year-end price; the ongoing trajectory and comparison with the May peak are not separate outcomes in its ladder.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether custom accelerators and easing GPU supply pressure the market price of compute\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a proposed cause of the price movement, not the tracked price figure.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": \"n/a\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"fail-control\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   36\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The figure covers 12 months after July 7, 2026, rather than calendar 2026, and the March 31, 2027 due day falls before that period ends.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The figure covers the 12 months after the 2026-07-07 survey (to mid-2027), not a calendar year, so year '2026' is not its period and the code-set due day 2027-03-31 falls before that period ends; with no stated publication day the step should have declined (D417).\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, and how far will Nvidia's share of the Chinese accelerator market fall from its present level?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budgets allocated to domestic products versus the ~30% baseline reported in the July 2026 Bloomberg Intelligence survey, and the direction of Nvidia's share of that market, as reported in follow-up surveys and Nvidia's disclosed China revenue.\",\n    \"period\": \"The 12 months following the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (through roughly mid-2027); set by the survey's stated 'next 12 months' horizon, checked against follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosures over that window.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys of Chinese AI buyers, Chinese customs/import statistics for AI accelerators, domestic vendors' (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon) reported revenue, and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosure in its 10-Q filings.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n    \"title\": \"Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators for domestic AI suppliers\",\n    \"text\": \"By Bloomberg\\nChinese companies are ditching Nvidia Corp.’s advanced accelerators in favor of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the AI infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\\nExecutives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday. In addition, 80% of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\\nChina’s biggest AI infrastructure builders and their key suppliers — Tencent Holdings Ltd., Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and Huawei Technologies Co. — are best-positioned to take advantage of the shift. AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology Co. and Cambricon Technologies Corp. were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\\n“China’s drive to substitute locally made AI-semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic markers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\nWhile Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\nChina is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) to build data centers across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management. At least 80% of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\nBut a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. with ChangXin Memory Technologies Inc. poised to reap the benefits. The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report.\\nBy Gao Yuan\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-08 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"ai-capex\",\n    \"kind\": \"theme\",\n    \"focus\": \"The share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budgets allocated to domestic products versus the ~30% baseline reported in the July 2026 Bloomberg Intelligence survey, and the direction of Nvidia's share of that market, as reported in follow-up surveys and Nvidia's disclosed China revenue.\",\n    \"question\": \"What percentage of Chinese enterprises’ AI accelerator budgets will be allocated to domestic products over the 12 months following the Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on 2026-07-07, as established by follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys?\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"below 38 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 38 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 38 percent to below 43 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 38 percent to below 43 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 43 percent to below 49 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 43 percent to below 49 percent (the reference when the question opened: \\\"Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 49 percent to below 54 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 49 percent to below 54 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"54 percent or more\",\n      \"condition\": \"54 percent or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"How far Nvidia’s share of the Chinese accelerator market will fall from its present level.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a separate market-share outcome, not the domestic-products budget share tracked by the question.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\",\n     \"kind\": \"expected\",\n     \"value\": \"46\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"n/a\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"fail-control\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   96\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The question asks for Q4 prices instead of the full-year price outcome; Q4 is not a whole-year figure, so the annual publication date is also wrong.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The question asks for the change from Q4 2026 to Q4 2027, which is one quarter's figure and not a whole year's; code's due day of 2028-03-30 (90 days after the calendar year-end) is only right for a full-year figure, so the due day fails too.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will DRAM/HBM contract prices remain elevated through calendar 2027, extending the shortage that has raised GPU makers' memory input costs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Direction and level of DRAM/HBM contract prices through calendar 2027, specifically whether they remain above 2026 levels or normalize as Micron's 2027/2028 outlook suggests.\",\n    \"period\": \"Full-year calendar 2027; checked against Q4 2027 DRAM contract price releases (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange) and memory makers' Q4 2027 results, reported roughly January 2028.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"TrendForce/DRAMeXchange quarterly and Q4 2027 DRAM contract price releases, plus Micron, Samsung and SK Hynix Q4 2027 results and supply commentary.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-12\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Skips New Gaming GPUs, Breaks 30-Year Streak [2026] - https://tech-insider.org/\",\n    \"text\": \"For the first time in close to three decades, Nvidia is not shipping a new gaming GPU architecture in a calendar year. Multiple reports confirm that, with September 2026 underway, the company has shipped zero new gaming GPUs all year and has nothing left on the calendar to change that — breaking a release streak that stretches back to the GeForce 256 ‘s debut in October 1999 . According to The Information, Nvidia quietly shelved its planned RTX 50 “Super” refresh back in December 2025 — including a 24GB RTX 5080 Super that had reportedly reached completed-design stage — and reporting corroborated through September 2026 confirms the company’s next-generation RTX 60-series remains pushed back to 2028. The reason has nothing to do with chip design and everything to do with memory: a global DRAM and HBM shortage that industry press has taken to calling “RAMageddon” is forcing Nvidia to funnel scarce memory supply toward its far more profitable AI accelerators instead of gaming cards.\\nNvidia Confirms No New Gaming GPUs Are Coming in 2026\\nWhat Happened to the RTX 5080 Super\\nTimeline: How the 2026 Gaming GPU Gap Unfolded\\nRTX 60-Series and Rubin: Pushed to 2028\\nEvery Major GeForce Architecture Since 1999\\nWhat the Q1 2026 Numbers Show\\nThe Software Workaround: DLSS, FSR, and Why They Matter More in 2026\\nDLSS 5 Launches September 3 in NBA 2K27\\nVRAM Is Still the Achilles’ Heel Without the 5080 Super\\n2026 vs. 2021: Why This Shortage Cuts Deeper\\nCould Nvidia Reverse Course Before 2028?\\nWill Nvidia Break Its Silence at CES 2027?\\nSignals Worth Watching Through the Rest of 2026\\nWhat Two More Years on the RTX 50-Series Means for Game Development\\nIs Nvidia really skipping every new gaming GPU in 2026?\\nHas Nvidia officially commented on the memory shortage or the GPU skip?\\nWhy is a memory shortage stopping GPU launches?\\nWhat happened to the RTX 5080 Super?\\nIs the RTX 5060 still available to buy?\\nWhat is DLSS 5 and when did it launch?\\nWhen will the RTX 60-series launch?\\nCould Nvidia unveil a surprise gaming GPU at CES 2027?\\nShould I still buy a gaming GPU in 2026?\\nHow long will the memory shortage last?\\nIs AMD better positioned than Nvidia right now?\\nDoes this affect laptop GPUs too?\\nThe story matters well beyond one product line. It marks the clearest sign yet that the AI buildout is actively cannibalizing the consumer hardware market it once merely inflated, and it reshapes the competitive picture for AMD and Intel, the used-GPU market, PC builders, and even console makers, right as the gaming GPU pricing surge Tech Insider has been tracking all year enters a new phase.\\nNvidia has shipped zero new gaming GPU architectures through September 2026 , breaking a release streak dating back roughly 30 years to the GeForce 256 in 1999.\\nThe planned RTX 5080 Super (24GB) reached a completed-design stage before Nvidia shelved the entire RTX 50 Super refresh in December 2025 .\\nThe next-generation RTX 60-series , built on the Rubin platform, has slipped from a rumored late-2027 launch to 2028 .\\nThe cause is a global memory shortage: Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have reportedly sold out their 2026 HBM output to AI data center customers.\\nThe RTX 5060 has been pulled from retail, with RTX 50-series production cut an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026.\\nNvidia’s most concrete gaming announcement of the year is software, not silicon: DLSS 5 launched September 3, 2026 , debuting in NBA 2K27 with day-one support on RTX 50-series GPUs and GeForce NOW .\\nThe report first surfaced via The Information in early February 2026 and was independently corroborated by outlets including PCWorld , TrendForce , PC Gamer , and the YouTube channel Moore’s Law Is Dead. The claim is specific: no new gaming GPU architecture will ship in 2026, a gap that breaks a release cadence stretching back nearly 30 years to the GeForce 256 ‘s October 1999 debut. Nvidia has shipped a new GeForce generation, refresh, or Super/Ti update virtually every year since, surviving the 2008 financial crisis, the 2017-2018 and 2021 crypto-mining booms, and the pandemic-era supply crunch without a full blank year. This is different. It is not a delay measured in months, it is an entire generation skipped outright.\\nNvidia has still not issued a full on-the-record confirmation of the 2026 gaming GPU gap, and the company continues to sell existing RTX 50-series inventory. Its only public acknowledgment to date remains the statement it gave to Tom’s Hardware confirming memory supply constraints, without confirming the broader skip outright. That, combined with the fact that it has not denied the original reporting, lines up with what AIB partners and retailers have quietly signaled for months: allocation for gaming SKUs has been shrinking while data center shipments accelerate.\\nThe decision comes down to margins and materials. According to reporting cited by PCWorld, Nvidia’s AI accelerators carry profit margins near 65% , compared with roughly 40% for consumer gaming GPUs. Every gigabyte of GDDR7 or HBM that goes into a GeForce card is a gigabyte that cannot go into a data center Blackwell or Rubin part selling for tens of thousands of dollars. When memory itself is the bottleneck, rather than fab capacity for the logic die, the highest-margin product wins the allocation fight every time.\\nThe shift shows up starkly in Nvidia’s own revenue mix. Gaming GPU revenue has shrunk to a small fraction of total company revenue as AI data center revenue has exploded around it, even as gaming revenue in absolute dollars has held relatively flat. A division that once defined the company is now a rounding error next to AI infrastructure, which makes the calculus behind skipping a gaming generation to protect memory supply for Blackwell Ultra and Vera Rubin parts far less surprising in hindsight.\\nThe clearest casualty is the RTX 5080 Super. According to The Information, as reported by Tom’s Hardware and Yahoo Finance, Nvidia completed the design for a 24GB GDDR7 refresh of the RTX 5080 — a card that would have addressed one of the current lineup’s biggest complaints, VRAM capacity that increasingly lags behind what 4K gaming and AI-assisted creative workloads demand — before shelving the entire RTX 50 Super refresh in December 2025 as memory supply was redirected to AI and data-center products. The card will not be manufactured, at least not in 2026, because the GDDR7 modules it needs are going to those higher-margin products instead.\\nIt is not just the Super refresh. Nvidia is reportedly cutting production of existing RTX 50-series cards already on shelves by an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026 , which helps explain why street prices on cards like the RTX 5070 Ti and RTX 5080 have stayed stubbornly above MSRP for most of 2026, a trend Tech Insider covered in depth in its look at the RTX 5090 approaching the $3,000 mark — a figure that now looks almost quaint. By September 2026, PCGamesN was tracking RTX 5090 listings above $6,000 , and TechRadar found even the cheapest available card selling for $6,389 , nearly 3.2 times its original $1,999 MSRP. The entry-level RTX 5060 has taken the hardest hit, reportedly pulled from retail entirely for at least six months . Fewer new cards shipping into a market where demand has not gone anywhere is a straightforward recipe for prices that refuse to soften.\\nPiecing together reporting from late 2025 through this month, the sequence of events looks like this:\\nDecember 2025 — Nvidia shelves the entire RTX 50 Super refresh, including a completed 24GB RTX 5080 Super design, redirecting memory supply toward AI and data-center products, according to The Information.\\nEarly February 2026 — The Information’s reporting becomes public and is independently corroborated by PCWorld, TrendForce, PC Gamer, and Moore’s Law Is Dead: no new gaming GPU architecture is coming in 2026, and the RTX 60-series slips from a rumored late-2027 launch to 2028.\\nJuly 2026 — Nvidia partially breaks its silence, acknowledging memory supply constraints in a statement to Tom’s Hardware without confirming the skip outright. The RTX 5060 remains pulled from retail, and supply-chain chatter continues to point to 2028 for the RTX 60-series.\\nAugust 2026 — The situation holds steady: Nvidia has shipped no new gaming architecture, the RTX 50 Super refresh remains shelved, and the RTX 60-series is still tracking to 2028, with no further on-the-record comment from Nvidia beyond its earlier Tom’s Hardware statement.\\nSeptember 2026 — Nvidia has still shipped no new gaming GPU architecture in 2026, the shelved RTX 50 Super refresh (including the completed-design 24GB RTX 5080 Super) remains dead, and the RTX 60-series continues to track to 2028, with Nvidia yet to move beyond its earlier Tom’s Hardware statement on memory constraints. The month’s one concrete gaming announcement was software, not silicon: DLSS 5 launched September 3 , debuting in NBA 2K27 with day-one support on RTX 50-series GPUs and GeForce NOW.\\nNvidia’s next true architectural leap for gaming, expected to be built on the Rubin platform that already anchors its next-gen AI roadmap, had been rumored for a late-2027 gaming launch. That timeline has reportedly slipped to 2028 , according to reporting from The Information corroborated by Tom’s Hardware and Yahoo Finance. For context, Nvidia moved from Ampere to Ada Lovelace to Blackwell in roughly two-year steps through the 2020s. A jump from Blackwell (RTX 50, launched 2025) to a Rubin-based RTX 60-series in 2028 would stretch that cadence to three years, matching the longest gap yet between GeForce architectures in the modern GPU era.\\nTo understand how unusual this is, it helps to see the cadence laid out generation by generation. Nvidia has launched a new gaming architecture or a significant refresh virtually every year since the GeForce 256 debuted in October 1999 , the card widely credited with kicking off the modern GPU era and, with it, this roughly 30-year annual-launch streak. 2026 is the first blank entry.\\nThe streak’s first decade is less discussed but equally unbroken. After the GeForce 256 debuted in October 1999 , Nvidia shipped the GeForce2 in 2000, the GeForce3 in 2001, and the GeForce4 in 2002, then moved to the GeForce FX (5-series) in 2003 and the GeForce 6 and 7 series in 2004 and 2005. The GeForce 8 series arrived in late 2006 as Nvidia’s first DirectX 10 part, followed by the GeForce 200-series in 2008 and the Fermi-based GeForce 400-series in 2010. Each of those transitions landed during periods that were not exactly friendly to consumer electronics spending, including the dot-com bust and the 2008 financial crisis, yet none of them produced a blank year the way 2026 now has.\\nThe modern cadence traces back through Kepler (GeForce GTX 600-series, 2012 ), Maxwell (GTX 900-series, 2014 ), and Pascal (GTX 10-series, 2016 ), before the naming shifted to RTX with Turing in 2018 . From there, Nvidia settled into the roughly two-year rhythm captured in the table below: Ampere in 2020 , Ada Lovelace in 2022 , and Blackwell in 2025 . Every one of those transitions, and every gap between them going back to the GeForce 256 itself, was bridged by at least a mid-cycle Super or Ti refresh to keep new SKUs landing on shelves. 2026 is the first calendar year in that entire span with nothing new at all, gaming architecture or refresh, which is what makes the 2028 wait for Rubin-based RTX 60-series cards land so differently for buyers than any previous gap.\\nShelved Dec. 2025, not manufactured\\nThe gap is the story. Every prior multi-year stretch between major GeForce architectures still came with some kind of mid-cycle refresh, a Super or Ti line, or a die-shrink update to keep the product line moving. 2026 has none of that for gaming, even as Nvidia’s AI lineup keeps shipping on schedule.\\nThe root cause sits one layer below Nvidia entirely. Hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon are buying up high-bandwidth memory as fast as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC’s reporting from January 2026. HBM demand is projected to grow roughly 70% year-over-year in 2026, and producing a single bit of HBM consumes an estimated three times the wafer capacity of standard DDR5, which means every wafer redirected toward AI memory removes capacity that would otherwise have gone into consumer DRAM, GDDR6, or GDDR7.\\nTesla CEO Elon Musk publicly acknowledged the squeeze in late January 2026, and Micron’s own leadership described current conditions as historically severe on the company’s February 2026 earnings call. Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 . Wikipedia’s running summary of the shortage is a useful neutral reference point for how the timeline has developed since it began in 2025.\\nTom’s Hardware has tracked DRAM contract pricing climbing by anywhere from roughly 60% to more than 90% quarter-over-quarter at different points in 2026, depending on the segment and the tracker consulted, even as some consumer retail prices have started to cool slightly as buyers hit their affordability ceiling. Major PC makers including Dell, HP, Lenovo, Acer, and Asus have separately warned of 15% to 20% price increases across their 2026 lineups, with some estimates running as high as 30% for memory-heavy configurations.\\nThe most recent quarterly numbers from Jon Peddie Research show just how dominant Nvidia remains even as its gaming roadmap stalls. Total discrete GPU shipments came in at 11.8 million units for Q1 2026, down 0.6% from the prior quarter, a soft but not collapsing figure given rising component and memory costs. By June 2026, shipments had swung the other way entirely: Tom’s Hardware reported desktop GPU shipments climbing to 12.5 million units , a four-year high, evidence that the production pullback has done little to cool actual demand even as prices keep climbing. Intel was the only vendor to gain share quarter-over-quarter, a small but symbolically important shift as Arc-series cards find a foothold in the budget segment.\\nOne more data point from the same report stands out: the GPU attach rate per desktop PC shipped jumped to 76% , up more than 33% from the prior quarter. Fewer people are skipping a discrete card entirely, even as prices rise, which suggests demand for dedicated gaming and AI-capable graphics is not softening nearly as fast as supply is.\\nThat attach-rate jump is worth sitting with, because it cuts against the intuitive assumption that a supply crunch pushes buyers toward integrated graphics or no upgrade at all. Instead, it suggests the opposite is happening at the margin: buyers who are in the market at all are choosing to prioritize a discrete GPU even as prices climb, rather than defer the purchase entirely, which is consistent with the used-market strength described below. It also helps explain why Nvidia’s board partners have had little incentive to discount aggressively despite a flat year for architecture news. Demand at the point of sale has not been the constraint. Supply has.\\nOn paper, a full year with no new Nvidia gaming GPU is the biggest opening AMD has had in years. The current Radeon RX 9000 lineup, built on RDNA 4, already competes well against Nvidia’s mid-range Blackwell cards, and Tech Insider’s own coverage of the AM5 platform’s extended lifespan shows AMD leaning into platform longevity as a selling point while Nvidia’s roadmap stalls. If AMD can get meaningfully more RDNA silicon into channel over the back half of 2026, it has a real shot at clawing back share from a rival that is, for once, not shipping anything new.\\nThe caveat is that AMD buys memory from the exact same three suppliers Nvidia does, and Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron are just as sold out to AMD’s own AI accelerator ambitions as they are to Nvidia’s. Intel faces a similar ceiling with Arc, though its smaller current footprint means the growth is coming off a much lower base, as reflected in that Q1 2026 uptick to 1% share. Neither rival can simply flood the market while the underlying memory shortage persists. At best, both get a slightly larger slice of a supply-constrained pie rather than a genuine open lane.\\nWith no new silicon arriving, the main performance lever Nvidia can still pull for existing RTX 50-series owners is software. DLSS upscaling and frame generation use AI to boost frame rates without new hardware, and driver-level improvements to those tools carry more weight in a year when the usual jump to a new generation simply is not happening. AMD is leaning on the same strategy with FSR on its RDNA 4-based Radeon RX 9000 cards, which means the competitive battle for the rest of 2026 is being fought largely in software rather than on new silicon from either company. For buyers, that shifts the practical question from “which new card should I wait for” to “which existing card has the better upscaling and frame-generation support today,” since that is the axis both companies can still meaningfully improve while memory constraints keep new hardware off the table.\\nThe single most concrete piece of Nvidia gaming news in 2026 arrived on September 3, 2026 , at 9 PM Pacific Time , when DLSS 5 launched with NBA 2K27 as its debut title. At launch, DLSS 5 is supported on GeForce RTX 50-series GPUs and through GeForce NOW , meaning every existing RTX 50-series owner gets Nvidia’s newest AI upscaling and frame-generation technology without buying anything new. That detail matters more than it would in a normal year: it is Nvidia’s biggest tangible gaming announcement of 2026, and it ships as a driver and software update to hardware that is already a year old rather than as a reason to buy a new GPU.\\nThe pattern extends across Nvidia’s other gaming appearances this year. At both Computex and Gamescom in 2026, the company’s own GeForce news pages centered on new RTX 50-series partner cards and incremental software features, including an earlier DLSS 4.5 update that Nvidia was still promoting on October 21, 2026 for current GeForce RTX products and upcoming games, rather than any new consumer gaming GPU architecture. DLSS 5 is simply the clearest version of that same story: Nvidia has real gaming news to share in 2026, it is just never the new silicon that would normally anchor a year like this one.\\nThe shelved RTX 5080 Super mattered because it targeted the lineup’s most persistent complaint: VRAM headroom that increasingly lags what 4K gaming and AI-assisted creative workloads demand. Without that refresh, current RTX 5080 owners are stuck with the original card’s VRAM configuration for a full extra generation cycle, since the earliest realistic replacement is now the 2028 Rubin-based RTX 60-series rather than a 2026 Super update. That makes VRAM the clearest example of a hardware limitation that software alone cannot fully paper over. DLSS and frame generation can stretch a GPU’s rendering performance, but they cannot manufacture memory capacity that was never installed on the board, which is why the 5080 Super’s cancellation lands harder for creative and high-resolution gaming workloads than for average 1440p play.\\nThe VRAM shortfall lands harder in 2026 than it would have a few years ago because gaming GPUs are no longer used only for gaming. RTX cards are the default entry point for anyone running local AI workloads, image generators, locally hosted large language models, and AI-assisted video and photo editing, on their own hardware instead of a cloud API. Those workloads are typically far more VRAM-hungry than modern game engines, since model weights and their associated caches have to fit entirely in memory to run at usable speed. The cancelled RTX 5080 Super , with its 24GB of GDDR7, would have specifically targeted that crowd, giving prosumer buyers a mid-tier option with meaningfully more headroom than the standard RTX 5080 at a lower price than the flagship RTX 5090. Without it, anyone who wants more VRAM than the base RTX 5080 offers has only one direction to go: the significantly pricier flagship, or waiting for the RTX 60-series in 2028 . That gap is a big part of why the 5080 Super’s cancellation registers as more than a gaming story. It removes the one card in Nvidia’s near-term roadmap that was built to answer the fastest-growing use case for a gaming GPU that has nothing to do with games.\\nFor anyone weighing a new build against Tech Insider’s own gaming PC build guide , the practical takeaway is straightforward: waiting for a better card is no longer a viable strategy in 2026. There is no RTX 5080 Super coming to justify holding off, and the RTX 60-series is now a 2028 story at the earliest. Whatever RTX 50-series or Radeon RX 9000 card fits your budget today is likely to remain the best available option in its price band for the rest of the year, and possibly into 2027.\\nThat also raises the stakes on components that are easy to get wrong under time pressure. Builders rushing a purchase before prices climb further should still budget extra care for details like proper 12VHPWR cable seating , since a shortage-driven rush job is exactly the scenario in which corners get cut on power connector installation.\\nWith no new flagship on the horizon to trigger the usual price cascade, where a new generation typically pushes prior-generation cards down sharply, the used market for RTX 40-series and even RTX 30-series cards has held value far better than a normal product cycle would predict. The pressure at the very top of the lineup has only sharpened that dynamic: TechRadar tracked third-party RTX 5090 listings surpassing $9,000 in September 2026, and pricing tracker Shattered.io logged peaks as high as $9,500 , roughly 4.75 times the card’s $1,999 MSRP. Listings that would typically depreciate 20-30% within a year of a new architecture launching are instead holding closer to their original resale value, because buyers priced out of new RTX 50-series stock, especially at the flagship tier, are competing for the same limited pool of older, still-capable cards. It is a dynamic that mirrors, in miniature, what happened during the 2021 crypto-mining shortage, except this time there is no clear end date tied to a mining difficulty adjustment or a coin’s price crashing.\\nGraphics silicon is not the only place memory scarcity is showing up in gaming hardware. Higher DRAM and NAND costs are pushing up bills of materials across the board, and reports have linked delays on other gaming-adjacent hardware, including Valve’s Steam Machine, to the same shortage. Console makers are not immune either: any mid-generation refresh hardware from Sony or Microsoft, and any future silicon destined for a next-generation console, will be competing for the exact same constrained pool of GDDR and DRAM that gaming GPUs, AI accelerators, and everyday laptops are all fighting over. Tech Insider’s coverage of Steam’s hardware survey trends already shows AMD silicon holding a substantial install-base share across PC and console alike, which means AMD’s own memory allocation constraints ripple into console economics as well as GPU economics.\\nGamers who lived through the 2020-2021 crypto-mining GPU shortage might assume this is a repeat. It is not, for one structural reason: the 2021 shortage was a demand-side shock. Miners wanted more of the exact same cards gamers wanted, and when mining profitability collapsed, the shortage ended almost overnight as miners dumped inventory back onto the market. The 2026 shortage is a supply-side reallocation. Memory manufacturers are not chasing a speculative bubble that can pop. They are fulfilling multi-year contracts with the largest, best-funded customers in the history of computing. There is no equivalent “crash” waiting to release that capacity back to consumer GPUs, which is precisely why Micron’s own leadership is talking about 2027 and 2028, not a few quarters, as the earliest realistic point for relief.\\nIt is worth asking directly: could Nvidia change its mind and rush out a gaming refresh before the RTX 60-series arrives in 2028? The facts assembled so far point to no, at least not on a timeline that would help anyone shopping in September 2026 . Memory manufacturers have reportedly locked in their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts with hyperscalers, not spot-market deals Nvidia could outbid on short notice, and the underlying margin math, roughly 65% for AI accelerators versus roughly 40% for gaming GPUs, does not change just because gamers are unhappy about it. A reversal would require either a meaningful loosening of memory supply or a strategic decision to sacrifice AI-accelerator margin to protect the gaming franchise, and nothing in the reporting so far suggests Nvidia is considering the latter.\\nThe more realistic path back to a normal release cadence is tied to when memory supply itself loosens, not to Nvidia reconsidering its priorities. Micron’s own leadership has pointed to 2027 as a plausible point for conditions to start easing, with a fuller return to balance by 2028 , which lines up closely with the RTX 60-series’ current target window. That timing is unlikely to be a coincidence: if memory supply genuinely normalizes on that schedule, 2028 is not just when Rubin-based silicon happens to be ready, it is also plausibly the first year Nvidia can build a full gaming lineup without cannibalizing its own AI allocation to do it.\\nThe next date worth circling is CES 2027 in Las Vegas each January, the show where Nvidia has unveiled major gaming hardware before, including the original RTX 50-series launch in January 2025. That history is exactly why the show matters here: if Nvidia is going to offer its most detailed public comment yet on the 2026 skip, or put a firmer date on the delayed RTX 60-series, CES is the venue with the built-in stage and audience to do it.\\nNothing in the reporting so far points to an actual surprise product launch. The same long-term memory contracts that forced the original skip were reportedly locked in well before any CES 2027 keynote gets written, so they do not reset just because the calendar flips to January. The realistic outcome is a status update rather than new hardware on stage: a firmer RTX 60-series timeline, acknowledgment of the RTX 5080 Super’s cancellation, or additional detail on memory supply would all count as meaningful news. An actual new gaming GPU announcement would contradict the reporting cited throughout this piece and would require memory allocation decisions that, by all accounts, were finalized more than a year earlier.\\nFor readers who want to track this themselves rather than wait for the next headline, a handful of recurring events double as check-ins on whether the picture is improving, holding steady, or getting worse:\\nMemory-maker earnings calls. Micron, Samsung, and SK Hynix all report quarterly, and each call is where supply commentary tends to surface first, exactly as it did on Micron’s February 2026 call.\\nNvidia’s own quarterly earnings. Nvidia has stopped short of a full on-the-record confirmation of the 2026 skip, but its earnings calls remain the venue most likely to produce a change in tone if one is coming.\\nTrendForce and Jon Peddie Research updates. Both outlets publish on a regular cadence, TrendForce on supply-chain shifts and JPR on quarterly shipment and market-share numbers, and either could show the first hard evidence of AMD or Intel gaining meaningful ground.\\nAIB partner and retailer allocation chatter. Because Nvidia itself has stayed largely quiet, board partners and retailers have been the earliest source for details like the RTX 5060’s retail pull, and are likely to be again if allocations shift.\\nCES 2027 in January. The first major date on next year’s calendar, and a show where Nvidia has made major gaming announcements before, making it the next high-visibility opportunity to update its position beyond the July statement to Tom’s Hardware.\\nNone of these guarantee an early answer, but together they are the same mix of sources that surfaced the original story in the first place, which makes them the most reliable places to watch for the next real update rather than speculation.\\nRTX 50-series and RX 9000 prices stay elevated through the rest of 2026. With no new architecture to trigger a normal price cascade, current-generation cards keep their value far longer than usual.\\nAMD gains a point or two of share, not a dramatic swing. Expect incremental movement in the JPR numbers over the next two to three quarters rather than a structural shift, since AMD faces the same memory ceiling as Nvidia.\\nCloud and rental GPU services see a demand bump. Priced-out upgraders are likely to lean harder on cloud gaming and rented compute, adding pressure to services like GeForce NOW right as Tech Insider has reported on its own tightening usage caps .\\nThe RTX 60-series launch, expected in 2028, arrives to unusually pent-up demand. A three-year gap between architectures means whatever ships next will face immediate, intense buying pressure.\\nConsole makers quietly adjust plans rather than announce anything. Expect memory-driven cost pressure to show up as smaller storage tiers, delayed refresh hardware, or higher prices on next-generation consoles rather than public acknowledgment of the shortage as the cause.\\nThe financial case for skipping 2026 is straightforward once the memory math is laid out, but the brand math is murkier, and it is the part Nvidia’s own statements have addressed the least. Gaming built Nvidia’s public identity long before AI did: GeForce is still the name most PC buyers associate with the company, even as gaming has shrunk to a fraction of total revenue next to Blackwell and Rubin data-center sales. A three-year gap between the RTX 50-series and the Rubin-based RTX 60-series, the longest stretch in the roughly 30-year run traced earlier in this piece, is not just a product story. It is a full news cycle Nvidia is handing to AMD and Intel by default.\\nEnthusiast press, YouTube reviewers, and retailer promotions are all built around new GPU launches. Skipping a year does not just remove a product, it removes the earned media that normally comes with one: benchmark videos, launch-day retailer front pages, and the wave of comparison content that shapes which brand a first-time PC builder lands on. Every one of those slots that goes unfilled by Nvidia in 2026 is a slot AMD’s Radeon RX 9000 lineup or Intel’s Arc cards can fill instead, at a moment when Tech Insider’s own coverage already shows AMD leaning into platform longevity as a selling point. None of this shows up on a quarterly earnings call, which is exactly why it is easy for a company as dominant in AI as Nvidia to underweight internally, even though it is the kind of opening rivals normally spend years trying to manufacture on their own.\\nThat is also why CES 2027 carries more weight than a typical keynote. It is not just the next opportunity for a firmer RTX 60-series date, as discussed above. It is Nvidia’s first real chance to control the narrative around a gap that, left unaddressed, competitors and reviewers will otherwise keep defining on their own terms through the rest of 2026.\\nGame studios plan technical targets years ahead of release, and those targets are built around whatever hardware buyers are actually expected to own when the game ships. A stable, long-lived top tier is normally something consoles provide and PC GPUs do not, since Nvidia’s roughly two-year cadence through Ampere, Ada Lovelace, and Blackwell kept shifting the ceiling every generation. With the RTX 60-series now pushed to 2028 rather than the rumored late-2027 window, the RTX 50-series becomes Nvidia’s longest-serving flagship tier across that entire stretch, closer to a console-style fixed target than anything PC developers have planned around in recent memory.\\nThat cuts two ways. A longer-lived ceiling means more of the buying audience will own RTX 50-series hardware by the time a demanding path-traced or AI-heavy title ships, which can make it easier for developers to justify building around DLSS 5 and similar AI rendering features rather than hedging against an install base fragmented across several product generations. But it also caps how far developers can push VRAM-hungry techniques, since the shelved 24GB RTX 5080 Super was the one near-term card that would have raised the realistic memory ceiling below the flagship RTX 5090. Without it, studios targeting anything above the standard RTX 5080’s memory configuration are effectively designing around 2025-era headroom for a full extra generation cycle.\\nIt also raises the stakes on DLSS 5 itself. Because it shipped as a driver-level update to existing RTX 50-series cards rather than alongside new silicon, DLSS 5 is effectively the only major rendering lever Nvidia and its development partners have to pull for the next two years, not the one or two a normal cycle would suggest. The same logic extends to consoles: since mid-generation refresh hardware from Sony and Microsoft faces the same memory constraints described earlier in this piece, PC and console capability may end up more closely aligned for longer than usual, which could make cross-platform optimization somewhat more predictable even as it narrows the ceiling for PC-exclusive showcase features through 2028.\\nStrip away the product-specific details and the pattern is simple: the same AI boom that has made Nvidia one of the most valuable companies on the planet is now directly limiting what it can sell to the gamers who built its original fanbase. Every HBM stack that goes into a data center accelerator is capacity that does not go into a GeForce card, and Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 . With Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron all reportedly sold out on 2026 HBM output, that tradeoff is not going away by itself. Gaming GPUs, once Nvidia’s flagship product, are now a secondary use for the memory supply it needs most for AI, and 2026 is the first year that tradeoff became impossible to paper over with a Super refresh or a die shrink.\\nMultiple outlets, including PCWorld and TrendForce, reported in February 2026 that Nvidia has no new gaming GPU architecture planned for release during the year, and that a planned RTX 5080 Super refresh — reportedly shelved in December 2025 , according to The Information — had been designed but would not be manufactured. Nvidia has not issued a full on-the-record confirmation or denial of the reporting, though the company has since acknowledged memory supply constraints in a statement to Tom’s Hardware .\\nOnly partially. Nvidia has not yet issued a full on-the-record confirmation of the 2026 gaming GPU skip, but the company did acknowledge memory supply constraints in a statement to Tom’s Hardware . That falls short of confirming the RTX 5080 Super cancellation or the RTX 60-series delay specifically, and it remains the closest Nvidia has come to addressing the reporting directly.\\nModern GPUs need GDDR6, GDDR7, or HBM memory alongside the graphics processor itself. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have reportedly sold out their 2026 HBM production to AI data center customers, leaving less capacity, and higher prices, for the memory that gaming cards also need.\\nReports from The Information indicate Nvidia completed the design for a 24GB GDDR7 version of the RTX 5080 but shelved the entire RTX 50 Super refresh in December 2025 and will not manufacture it in 2026, because the memory it requires is being allocated to higher-margin AI products instead.\\nAvailability has tightened sharply. Nvidia has reportedly pulled the RTX 5060 from the market entirely, with supply-chain sources pointing to at least six months before it returns, as part of a broader 15% to 20% production cut across the RTX 50-series through at least Q3 2026.\\nDLSS 5 launched on September 3, 2026 at 9 PM Pacific Time , debuting in NBA 2K27 with day-one support on GeForce RTX 50-series GPUs and GeForce NOW . It is Nvidia’s biggest tangible gaming announcement of 2026, and notably it is a software and driver update for existing hardware rather than a new GPU.\\nCurrent reporting points to 2028 for a Rubin-based RTX 60-series, a delay from earlier expectations of a late-2027 launch. That would make it roughly three years since the RTX 50-series debuted in 2025, matching the longest gap yet between GeForce architectures in recent history.\\nIt’s unlikely to be a full product launch. CES 2027 in January is the next high-visibility venue where Nvidia has a history of major gaming announcements, but the long-term memory contracts behind the 2026 skip were reportedly locked in well before the show, so the more realistic outcome is a status update on the RTX 60-series timeline or memory supply rather than new hardware on stage.\\nIf you need one, waiting is unlikely to pay off. With no new architecture coming and current-generation cards holding their value due to constrained supply, today’s RTX 50-series or Radeon RX 9000 pricing is a reasonable baseline for the rest of the year rather than a temporary peak before a better deal arrives, and at the flagship end that baseline has already jumped sharply: PCGamesN was tracking RTX 5090 listings above $6,000 in September 2026, more than triple its original $1,999 MSRP.\\nEstimates vary. Micron’s leadership has pointed to 2027 as a likely point for tightness to ease, with a fuller return to balance by 2028, while other analysts, including Kearney’s PERLab, have projected the crunch could persist as late as 2030.\\nAMD has an opportunity in that it is still shipping current-generation Radeon RX 9000 cards while Nvidia’s gaming roadmap has stalled, but AMD sources memory from the same three suppliers that are sold out to AI customers, so its ability to flood the gap is limited rather than unlimited.\\nYes. The same memory constraints pushing up desktop GPU prices are also raising bills of materials for laptops, and PC makers including Dell, HP, Lenovo, Acer, and Asus have all warned of price increases across their 2026 lineups, in some cases as high as 30% on memory-heavy configurations.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-12 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"semiconductor-cycle\",\n    \"kind\": \"theme\",\n    \"focus\": \"Direction and level of DRAM/HBM contract prices through calendar 2027, specifically whether they remain above 2026 levels or normalize as Micron's 2027/2028 outlook suggests.\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the year-over-year percentage change in DRAM/HBM contract prices be in Q4 2027 versus Q4 2026, as shown by TrendForce/DRAMeXchange’s Q4 2027 releases and memory makers’ Q4 2027 results?\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2028-03-30\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"below -10 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"below -10 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from -10 percent to below 0 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from -10 percent to below 0 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 0 percent to below 15 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 0 percent to below 15 percent (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 15 percent to below 35 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 15 percent to below 35 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"35 percent or more\",\n      \"condition\": \"35 percent or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the prices extend the shortage that has raised GPU makers’ memory input costs.\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a causal consequence of the price level, not the price figure being tracked.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether prices normalize as Micron’s 2027/2028 outlook suggests.\",\n      \"why\": \"The question measures the price change against Q4 2026, not whether it matches Micron’s outlook.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": \"n/a\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"fail-control\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/run-rechecked\",\n   95\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The share going to domestic Chinese suppliers is not the listed AI capital-spending theme, and the expected publication day is the same day the measured period ends.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"D fails: the due day 2027-07-07 is the last day of the very 12 months the question measures (2026-07-07 to 2027-07-07), so no survey estimate of that window can have been published by then, and 'expected_publication' does not truly describe a day that is the period's end.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI-accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (e.g. Huawei Ascend and DeepSeek's in-house chips) over the next 12 months, and how much will that displace NVIDIA's China demand?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese AI-accelerator budgets allocated to domestic suppliers, versus roughly 30% today, as reported in industry surveys/market research and reflected in NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue.\",\n    \"period\": \"12 months from the item (by ~mid-2027), per the item's statement that Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Industry surveys/market-research estimates of Chinese AI-accelerator spending shares and NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue over the next 12 months (to mid-2027).\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence\",\n    \"text\": \"DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]\\nU.S. export controls bar Chinese designers from accessing the most advanced overseas foundries and high-bandwidth memory, posing a major manufacturing hurdle [2]\\nDeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.\\nThe project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2] . The company is in discussions with chip design firms, semiconductor foundries, and memory suppliers, though no specific partners have been named.\\nThe move marks a significant strategic expansion for DeepSeek, which burst onto the global AI stage in January 2025 with models that rivaled U.S. frontrunners at a fraction of the compute cost. It comes weeks after the company closed a record $7.5 billion funding round — its first outside capital — at a valuation exceeding $50 billion, with backing from Tencent, CATL, and founder Liang Wenfeng himself [4] .\\nDeepSeek's chip targets the fastest-growing segment of AI computing. Inference workloads — running trained models for end users — now account for the majority of AI chip demand as companies deploy models at scale. Custom inference chips are typically cheaper and more power-efficient than the general-purpose GPUs sold by Nvidia, making them attractive for companies seeking to compress costs [2] .\\nThe strategy aligns with DeepSeek's broader cost-efficiency playbook. In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5] . Controlling its own silicon would give DeepSeek further leverage to undercut rivals on inference pricing.\\nDesigning a competitive AI chip typically takes years and billions of dollars in investment. For a Chinese company, the challenge is compounded by U.S. export controls that bar access to the most advanced overseas foundries and restrict shipments of high-bandwidth memory — a component critical to inference chip performance [2] .\\nAnalyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3] .\\nChinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] . DeepSeek's chip effort fits squarely within that trend.\\nDeepSeek is not the only AI lab pursuing proprietary silicon. OpenAI and Anthropic have both disclosed custom chip programs, while Google has long run its TPU chips for both training and inference. Amazon designs its Trainium and Inferentia chips for AWS customers [2] .\\nWithin China, Huawei's Ascend line of AI accelerators has emerged as the primary domestic alternative to Nvidia. DeepSeek's in-house effort positions the startup as a competitor to Huawei in inference hardware — not just in AI models [5] .\\nNo timeline for tape-out or production has been disclosed. The project's early-stage status means a finished chip is likely years away, and manufacturing constraints under current U.S. export rules represent a structural bottleneck [2] .\\nDeepSeek's $7.5 billion war chest gives it the capital to sustain a multi-year hardware development effort. Founder Liang Wenfeng personally contributed roughly $2.9 billion to the round, and the deal's unusual structure — a limited partnership with a five-year lock-up for most investors — suggests the company is planning on a long horizon [4] .\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-07 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"ai-capex\",\n    \"kind\": \"theme\",\n    \"focus\": \"The share of Chinese AI-accelerator budgets allocated to domestic suppliers, versus roughly 30% today, as reported in industry surveys/market research and reflected in NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue.\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese companies’ AI-accelerator budgets will go to domestic suppliers over the 12 months from 2026-07-07 to 2027-07-07, as estimated by industry surveys or market research?\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-07-07\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"below 30 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 30 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 30 percent to below 40 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 30 percent to below 40 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 40 percent to below 50 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 40 percent to below 50 percent (the reference when the question opened: \\\"Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"from 50 percent to below 60 percent\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 50 percent to below 60 percent\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"60 percent or more\",\n      \"condition\": \"60 percent or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"How much that shift will displace NVIDIA’s China demand\",\n      \"why\": \"This is a separate company-demand outcome; the topic’s stated outcome tracks the domestic suppliers’ share of Chinese AI-accelerator budgets.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today\",\n     \"value\": \"46\"\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"n/a\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"fail-control\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/run-2\",\n   89\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The announced obligation is due at the next annual filing, not March 5, 2028, and the reference omits the announced $12.9 billion deal value.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"D: the thesis opens as binding_obligation and the $12.9 billion purchase was already agreed when the article appeared, so the question is due at the next annual filing (the FY2027 report, around 2027-02), and the due day 2028-03-05 is another day.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will Nvidia complete its announced $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, the open-source AI model repository, by a check date within 18 months of the September 2026 announcement?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Nvidia closes (or abandons/restructures) the announced $12.9 billion purchase of Hugging Face, an acquisition that would absorb cash and add a developer-platform asset.\",\n    \"period\": \"Check date within 18 months of the 2026-09-05 item (by approximately 2028-03-05); the item gives no closing date.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's Form 8-K and subsequent Form 10-Q/10-K reporting completion or termination of the Hugging Face acquisition, plus regulatory clearance announcements.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-05\",\n    \"title\": \"John McGuire bought NVIDIA stock before NVIDIA made a $12.9 billion AI deal\",\n    \"text\": \"MAGA Congressman John McGuire , Mr. “I Don’t Even Trades,” bought between $1,001 and $15,000 of NVIDIA stock ahead of NVIDIA’s announcement this week that it is buying AI startup Hugging Face .\\nThis is a matter of public record; McGuire filed a disclosure form on Wednesday noting the purchase with the Office of the Clerk of the House of Representatives .\\nThe purchase was made on Aug. 18 , per the disclosure.\\nOn Thursday, NVIDIA – market cap: $5.55 trillion – announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face, a New York-based startup that has built a repository for open-source AI models.\\nHugging Face was the focus of an intense bidding war given its place in the AI sphere – it has more than 18 million developers and researchers using more than 3 million models, and more than 200,000 enterprise customers.\\nMcGuire would know better than the rest of us just how valuable Hugging Face was on the open market because he serves on House Oversight subcommittees focused on cybersecurity, information technology and military affairs .\\nAssuming McGuire pays attention to what goes on in the subcommittee meetings, he’s getting access to classified and other sensitive information that the rest of us aren’t privy to, including the latest developments in who wants to buy who – valuable information, if you’re in a position to trade on it, like a congressman would be.\\nIt’s one thing to have access to classified and sensitive information; entirely another thing to take that information straight to the bank.\\nOf note here: McGuire, oddly, doesn’t seem to want to take credit for using the inside info to better his own personal bottom line.\\nAsked by a Lynchburg TV station last month about his reluctance to schedule a debate with his Democratic opponent, Tom Perriello , McGuire made the reluctance about Perriello having accused him of “insider trading,” claiming: “I don’t even do trades.”\\nInstead of patting himself on the back after getting what you could look at as praise from your opponent for having the sense to make money off what you know, McGuire signaled that he expected an apology from Perriello for pointing out the publicly available information about his stock trades.\\nWeird, right?\\nMaybe he’s telling us there that he gets it, that it doesn’t look good, after all.\\nCredit here on the stock trades becoming public knowledge in the first place goes to Cville Right Now , which broke the story on McGuire actually doing trades , reporting back on July 13 that McGuire, after voting with the House majority to pass the 21st Century Road to Housing Act “sold off over $1,000 of BlackRock Inc. stock , partially divesting himself from one of the companies the legislation targets three days before the bill became law,” and “also divulged purchasing between $1,001 and $15,000 in Space Explorations Technologies Corp ., the company founded by Elon Musk in 2002.”\\nThat information comes from a public disclosure form on file with the Office of the Clerk of the U.S. House of Representatives – filed by McGuire, with his signature.\\nThe BlackRock Inc. stock dump was dated July 7 ; the SpaceX dump was dated June 15 .\\nHere’s where I added to the reporting done last month by CRN : Mr. “I Don’t Even Do Trades” dumped more BlackRock stock – in separate trades valued between $1,001 and $15,000 – on July 10 and July 14 , per a filing made with the clerk’s office on Aug. 7 .\\nThat Aug. 7 filing also includes a notation of a sale of between $1,001 and $15,000 in stock of Applied Materials Inc ., a Silicon Valley company – market cap: $360.9 billion – that supplies equipment, services and software for the manufacture of semiconductor chips for electronics, flat panel displays for computers, smartphones, televisions, and solar products.\\nOne other from the Aug. 7 filing: the sale of between $1,001 and $15,000 of stock in Palo Alto Networks Inc. , another Silicon Valley company – market cap: $271.6 billion – that focuses on cybersecurity, with its core product being a platform that includes advanced firewalls and cloud-based offerings that extend those firewalls to cover other aspects of security.\\nYou’d think he’d be proud of himself for all of this.\\nThe guy is $390,000 better off because he “does trades.”\\nMe, personally, I’d be bragging about that.\\nChris Graham is the founder and editor of Augusta Free Press . A 1994 alum of the University of Virginia , Chris is the author and co-author of seven books, including Poverty of Imagination , a memoir published in 2019. For his commentaries on news, sports and politics, go to his YouTube page , TikTok , BlueSky , or subscribe to his Street Knowledge podcast . Email Chris at [email protected] . Support AFP by signing up for our newsletter and/or powering our work with a one-time or monthly contribution .\\nJMU rallied from 10 down in the final 3:56 to force OT, but VCU’s Blake Harper poured in 11 of his game-high 30 points in the extra period to help the Rams to a 92-87 win over the Dukes on Sunday in a preseason contest at the Siegel Center. VCU led by 10 with 3:56...\\nIndiana native Kyle Guy helped the UVA Basketball program win the NCAA title in 2019, and he was voted the Most Outstanding Player in the NCAA Tournament that year.\\nWe have reached 57 percent of what we need to fund critical operations for 2026. If you have been waiting for the right time to support AFP , this is it.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"Whether Nvidia closes (or abandons/restructures) the announced $12.9 billion purchase of Hugging Face, an acquisition that would absorb cash and add a developer-platform asset.\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its announced acquisition of Hugging Face by March 5, 2028?\",\n    \"year\": \"2028\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2028-03-05\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"NVIDIA completes the announced acquisition of Hugging Face by March 5, 2028.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n      \"condition\": \"After September 5, 2026, the announced acquisition is formally abandoned, withdrawn, terminated, or blocked by a regulator, or the parties formally restructure it so that the announced acquisition will not close. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole taken after 2026-09-05, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole after that day, stands on 2028-03-05, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Still open\",\n      \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-05.\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face\",\n     \"value\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": null,\n    \"favourable_why\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"fail-control\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-fresh-a2-2026-11-16/choices/reading\",\n   12\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"The agreed acquisition is an obligation already undertaken and is due at the next annual filing, not March 24, 2028, and the article states an agreement to acquire Hugging Face that should be recorded as an expected reference.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"fails\",\n    \"why\": \"D: the acquisition is already agreed, an obligation taken on, so as a binding_obligation it is due at NVIDIA's next annual filing (the FY2027 annual report in early 2027), not 2028-03-24; H: termination by NVIDIA or Hugging Face is exactly what the Ruled out rung decides.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n    \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 8-K/press release announcing completion (or termination) of the Hugging Face acquisition, and the purchase-price and goodwill disclosures in its Form 10-Q/10-K.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n    \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\",\n    \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-25 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\",\n    \"year\": \"2028\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2028-03-24\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n      \"condition\": \"After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Still open\",\n      \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The acquisition's approximately $12.9 billion price, including whether it is repriced.\",\n      \"why\": \"The tracked outcome is whether the acquisition is completed by the check date, regardless of its final price.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether NVIDIA or Hugging Face terminates the acquisition agreement before the check date\",\n      \"why\": \"Termination is a different outcome from completion.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": null,\n    \"favourable_why\": null,\n    \"ruled_out_by\": \"NVIDIA, Hugging Face, or a regulator with authority to block the acquisition\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"hold-stated\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   28\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system begin shipping to customers by the end of calendar 2028, following SemiAnalysis's report that its debut slipped from 2027 to 2028 (which NVIDIA disputes, saying its roadmap is intact)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028-12-31 (the item says the Kyber debut was pushed to 2028 from a 2027 plan; the check date is the end of that year. If NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact,' shipping in 2027 would also settle the by-end-2028 question affirmatively.)\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA confirmation of first shipments of Vera Rubin Ultra/Kyber systems to cloud customers, or an explicit product-timeline disclosure in NVIDIA's annual report or quarterly earnings materials.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"text\": \"Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\\n\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\nThat leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\nThe delays \\\"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\\\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \\\"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\\\"\\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \\\"will potentially gain some time,\\\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \\\"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\\\" he said.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun by December 31, 2028?\",\n    \"year\": \"2028\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"By December 31, 2028, NVIDIA confirms that first customer shipments of the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n      \"condition\": \"After July 5, 2026 and before December 31, 2028, NVIDIA formally cancels the Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 system or abandons plans to ship it to customers. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2028-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Still open\",\n      \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-12-31.\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"SemiAnalysis's report that the debut slipped from 2027 to 2028\",\n      \"why\": \"The tracked outcome is first customer shipments by the deadline, not whether SemiAnalysis's reported delay is accurate.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"NVIDIA's dispute of that report, saying its roadmap is intact\",\n      \"why\": \"The tracked outcome is confirmed shipments by the deadline, not whether NVIDIA disputes or supports the reported delay.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027\",\n     \"kind\": \"expected\",\n     \"value\": \"2027\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": null,\n    \"favourable_why\": null,\n    \"ruled_out_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"hold-stated\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-carried-run-2026-11-16/stage-a\",\n   4\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"The article states that Nvidia proposed guaranteeing hardware residual values and sharing up to $125 billion in risk, but the reference is null.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will Nvidia finalize its proposed sharing of up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values for its AI infrastructure financing platform by the check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\",\n    \"period\": \"Check date 3 April 2028 (18 months after the 3 October 2026 item), with earlier disclosure possible in Nvidia's quarterly or annual filings.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's definitive agreement or SEC filing (10-Q/10-K) disclosing the finalized residual-value guarantee amount and terms for its AI infrastructure financing platform.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-10-03\",\n    \"title\": \"Amazon Pursues $8 Billion Sale-and-Leaseback of Nvidia Chips\",\n    \"text\": \"Amazon Pursues $8 Billion Sale-and-Leaseback of Nvidia Chips\\nAmazon is pursuing a sale-leaseback structure for approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia AI chips, transferring them to outside investors while continuing to use them. The company plans to raise funds through bond issuance via a special purpose vehicle, with Amazon retaining use of the chips. The move is seen as a strategy to relieve AI investment burdens from its balance sheet amid capital expenditures that have ballooned to $220 billion this year. As the AI industry increasingly treats semiconductors as financial assets, the deal—if completed—could trigger similar asset securitization moves by other Big Tech companies.\\nAmazon is pursuing a sale-leaseback structure for approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips, transferring them to outside investors while continuing to lease and use them. The strategy aims to relieve the surging burden of AI infrastructure investment from its balance sheet while maintaining data center computing capacity.\\nAccording to the Financial Times, Amazon has recently been in contact with investors to discuss transferring approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia \\\"Grace Blackwell\\\" chips deployed in U.S. data centers to a special purpose vehicle (SPV). The assets include thousands of AI chips installed across more than a dozen data centers in five U.S. states, including Nevada and Virginia.\\nThe structure works as follows: Amazon transfers the chips it has secured to an SPV, which then issues bonds to raise capital from outside investors. Amazon subsequently leases the chips back and continues to use them in its existing data centers. This allows the company to reduce the burden of carrying expensive AI chips directly on its books while maintaining the computing capacity it needs.\\nAmazon is reportedly discussing offering investors up to 10% equity in the vehicle while retaining no equity stake itself. Bond investors are expecting investment-grade ratings based on Amazon's double-A credit rating rather than the residual value of the hardware. This is a key factor enabling participation from conservative institutional capital such as insurers and pension funds.\\nIf the deal closes, Amazon would be able to offload approximately $8 billion in asset exposure while maintaining use of its AI chips. Big Tech faces a dilemma: it cannot halt investment in the AI race, but carrying the full cost on its balance sheet is burdensome—and that tension is now driving new financial engineering.\\nAstronomical Investment Costs Spur Financial Innovation\\nThe backdrop to Amazon's resort to this financing method is the astronomical growth in AI investment costs. Amazon's projected capital expenditures for this year stand at approximately $220 billion, up about 10% from the previous forecast of $200 billion. The surge is driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure, centered on its cloud business, Amazon Web Services (AWS).\\nThe primary driver of the upward revision is rising memory prices for AI servers. Demand continues to outstrip supply. AWS's backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\\nAndy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, said in July that even with significantly increased investment, the company would struggle to meet all AI computing demand.\\nAt the time, he stated: \\\"We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027.\\\" He added that \\\"the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary.\\\" The sale-leaseback structure is seen as a compromise to address the demand pressure he referenced while preserving financial soundness.\\nAcross the AI industry, there is a growing movement to treat semiconductors as a form of financial asset. Companies are borrowing against expensive GPUs and data centers as collateral, or outside investors are purchasing assets that Big Tech then leases on a long-term basis. Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms.\\nIn August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\\nRecent major financing deals using similar structures include:\\nPartnership with 6 financial firms\\nAmazon's move is part of a broader trend of converting AI infrastructure into financial products. The structure—where physical assets like semiconductors and data centers serve as underlying assets for bond issuance—resembles past aircraft leasing or real estate securitization, but carries differentiated risk given the rapid pace of technological obsolescence in AI chips.\\nAs Nvidia's chip generation cycles accelerate, there are concerns that the value of assets held by SPVs could decline faster than expected.\\nGrace Blackwell is Nvidia's cutting-edge chip, but the next-generation \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" is slated to succeed it. Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years. However, the structure—in which Amazon guarantees long-term use as lessee—provides investors with a degree of cash flow stability.\\nMarket observers believe that if this deal closes, other Big Tech companies are likely to adopt similar approaches. With AI infrastructure investment now measured in the hundreds of billions of dollars, asset securitization offers a practical alternative for improving capital efficiency.\\nAmazon has not issued an official statement regarding the specific terms of the deal or the list of investors. However, industry observers interpret the structure as a strategic choice by Big Tech to simultaneously achieve two goals: expanding AI investment and maintaining financial soundness.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-03 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia sign and disclose a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by 2028-04-03?\",\n    \"year\": \"2028\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2028-04-03\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"By 2028-04-03, Nvidia has entered into a definitive hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform and disclosed its finalized terms in a definitive agreement or SEC filing.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n      \"condition\": \"After 2026-10-03 and before 2028-04-03, Nvidia formally abandons or withdraws the proposed hardware residual-value guarantee without finalizing it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-03 and stands on 2028-04-03, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Still open\",\n      \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-04-03.\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The amount of the finalized guarantee, including whether it is reduced from the proposed amount of up to $125 billion.\",\n      \"why\": \"The judged outcome is whether Nvidia finalizes and discloses the guarantee, not the amount finalized.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": null,\n    \"favourable_why\": null,\n    \"ruled_out_by\": \"Nvidia\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"hold-stated\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   30\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"The article says Vera Rubin could begin shipping later this year, but the expected reference is null.\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"The article says Vera Rubin 'is in full production and could begin shipping later this year', a stated expectation for this event, yet the reference is null.\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin shipping by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2026-12-31 (the item says shipping could begin later in 2026, from a 2026-07-02 article)\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's confirmation of first customer shipments of Vera Rubin, such as a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n    \"title\": \"Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?\",\n    \"text\": \"Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article will explain why I like Microsoft a tad more. Although, as you'll see below, it's hard to go wrong with either one.\\nVera Rubin likely means Nvidia has more growth ahead\\nNvidia has enjoyed one of the most impressive growth spurts in history over these past few years as the AI build-out juiced demand for its GPUs. This supercycle continues to rage on. Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\nWall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. Nvidia's valuation is already well below its high at less than 19 times sales, and it might become much cheaper as these next 24 months play out.\\nMicrosoft is pivoting to capitalize on its enterprise customer base\\nMicrosoft originally tied its AI hopes to OpenAI. Although Microsoft has done well with its OpenAI investment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years.\\nWhile some may opt for Nvidia's immense growth potential , Microsoft rarely offers a valuation compelling enough to buy into.\\nJustin Pope is a contributing Motley Fool stock market analyst covering information technology, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, and industrials. Prior to The Motley Fool, Justin was a business manager for an industrial company.\\nBy Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-02 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that it has made first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform by December 31, 2026?\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"NVIDIA confirms first customer shipments of Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n      \"condition\": \"NVIDIA formally cancels Vera Rubin customer shipments for 2026 or announces that it will not ship Vera Rubin to customers by December 31, 2026. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-02 and stands on 2026-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Still open\",\n      \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-12-31.\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": null,\n    \"favourable_why\": null,\n    \"ruled_out_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"hold-stated\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/probe-second-look\",\n   66\n  ],\n  \"first_readers\": {\n   \"codex\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   },\n   \"claude\": {\n    \"D\": \"holds\",\n    \"why\": \"\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 check date — the item (published 2026-07-05) says Kyber was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 and has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, so end-2027 discriminates the original schedule from the reported delay.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's own shipment and roadmap disclosures — GTC keynote/product briefings, quarterly earnings commentary on Rubin Ultra revenue timing, and cloud-partner (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud) deployment confirmations — checked at year-end 2027.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"text\": \"Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\\n\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\nThat leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\nThe delays \\\"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\\\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \\\"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\\\"\\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \\\"will potentially gain some time,\\\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \\\"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\\\" he said.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA make a first shipment of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system paired with Vera Rubin Ultra by December 31, 2027, as confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement?\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Yes\",\n      \"condition\": \"NVIDIA discloses, or officially announces, a first shipment of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system paired with Vera Rubin Ultra by December 31, 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n      \"condition\": \"After July 5, 2026, NVIDIA formally abandons the Kyber NVL144 system or announces that it will not ship the system by December 31, 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Still open\",\n      \"condition\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the system ships in 2027 “as originally scheduled.”\",\n      \"why\": \"The original schedule is a comparison point; the judged outcome is whether a first shipment occurs by the due date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether Kyber has specifically slipped to 2028, as SemiAnalysis reports.\",\n      \"why\": \"The question does not separately judge the reported delay or its timing; no first shipment by the due date leaves the event unachieved, regardless of whether shipment is later expected in 2028.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027\",\n     \"kind\": \"expected\",\n     \"value\": \"2027\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": null,\n    \"favourable_why\": null,\n    \"ruled_out_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"stated-kept\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-ladder-stage-a-2026-11-10/probe-second-look\",\n   17\n  ],\n  \"constructed\": \"article + \\\"NVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\\\"; due day 2027-02-24, basis stated\",\n  \"first_readers\": null,\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much will Nvidia's commitments to suppliers (purchase obligations, primarily memory procurement) be at its next annual report, after rising to $279 billion in the second quarter from $119 billion the prior quarter?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported amount of Nvidia's commitments to suppliers / purchase obligations disclosed at its next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report: Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027 (filed in early 2027); set by the rule that an obligation already undertaken is tracked at the next annual report.\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's next annual report on Form 10-K, including its disclosure of purchase commitments and contractual obligations to suppliers.\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Recent News From Nvidia and SK Hynix Reveals Exactly What the Market Expects for Micron Technology's Future\",\n    \"text\": \"Micron Technology ( MU +4.20% ) has seen its revenue and profits soar amid booming demand for AI compute, and it could see its pockets get even fatter over the next few years based on recent news from its fellow AI chipmakers . Both Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) , which uses memory chips like Micron's in its GPU systems, and SK Hynix ( SKHY +4.48% ) , a rival memory chipmaker, announced news suggesting the memory chip supply shortage could last much longer.\\nThe market's reaction to the recent developments provides a clear indication of what the market expects for Micron going forward. Here's what investors need to know.\\nNvidia just made a huge commitment to memory chips\\nNvidia's second-quarter earnings report included a small detail that could have a huge impact on Micron and the rest of the memory chip industry. The company increased its commitments to suppliers to $279 billion, up from $119 billion in the previous quarter. The $160 billion increase is primarily due to memory procurement, CFO Colette Kress wrote in her prepared statement accompanying the earnings release.\\nNvidia likely signed long-term agreements with one or more memory chip suppliers. All three leading chipmakers started signing strategic agreements to guarantee demand well into the future in exchange for locking in prices today. That gives the companies the confidence to build new capacity with a guaranteed buyer at the end of the day. However, it caps how high prices can climb if demand growth continues to outpace supply growth.\\nInvestors have generally seen the long-term agreements as a bullish sign for the memory chipmakers. The guaranteed revenue could reduce the cyclicality that has historically plagued memory chip stocks.\\nSo, the fact that Nvidia made a huge commitment should be a positive signal for Micron stock. Nonetheless, the market didn't seem to react to the news; shares dropped 0.3% the day after Nvidia's earnings release.\\nSK Hynix's management says the memory shortage can last much longer\\nAt a press conference following the groundbreaking ceremony for SK Hynix's new Indiana manufacturing facility, CEO Kwak Noh-jung said the current memory supply shortage could last through 2030. SK Hynix's Indiana facility isn't set to begin mass production until the second half of 2029, and with a $4 billion price tag, a lot is riding on the continuation of the tight memory chip market.\\nMore importantly, the analyst consensus has been that supply will catch up to demand by 2028 and revenue growth will slow for the memory chipmakers. Micron's most recent guidance was that tight conditions will \\\"persist beyond calendar 2027.\\\"\\nShares of Micron barely budged on news that SK Hynix's management now expects a favorable market for its products to last through the end of the decade, driven by the unprecedented AI compute build-out .\\nWhat the market's reaction says about Micron stock\\nDespite positive developments or insider commentary on the memory market, Micron stock has barely moved. That suggests the market is already extremely optimistic about Micron Technology's future.\\nThat puts shareholders in a precarious position. Good news will have practically no effect on the stock price, as we saw at the end of August. Micron needs to release news that absolutely blows away expectations to move higher. While it's been done repeatedly over the last year or so, the market's expectations are now sky-high.\\nOn the flip side, any indication that the current earnings cycle won't be as strong as expected or won't last as long as forecast could be devastating for shareholders. The volatile stock could tumble lower on even a hint of bad news because expectations are so high.\\nInvestors may view the stock trading at just 6 times forward earnings as a relatively low-risk opportunity, but that's not the case for a cyclical stock like Micron. There's a lot of uncertainty in those earnings forecasts. A shortfall in earnings relative to expectations could reduce both earnings and the earnings multiple, compounding the downward impact on the stock price.\\nOf course, exceeding those expectations could have the opposite effect. It's just become increasingly difficult for Micron to do that.\\nBy Adam Levy – Sep 5, 2026 at 7:45AM EST\\nNVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The reported amount of Nvidia's commitments to suppliers / purchase obligations disclosed at its next annual report.\",\n    \"question\": \"What amount of Nvidia’s supplier commitments (purchase obligations, primarily memory procurement) will Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K, as of fiscal year-end?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-02-24\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 200,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 200,000,000,000 USD to below 250,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 250,000,000,000 USD to below 310,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \\\"The company increased its commitments to suppliers to $279 billion\\\")\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 310,000,000,000 USD to below 400,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"400,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"The commitments rose to $279 billion in the second quarter from $119 billion in the prior quarter.\",\n      \"why\": \"Those quarterly amounts and their comparison are not the amount tracked; the question is about the commitments disclosed in the next annual report.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": {\n     \"words\": \"The company increased its commitments to suppliers to $279 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"last_reported\",\n     \"value\": \"279000000000\",\n     \"value_to\": null\n    },\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"Lower supplier commitments mean fewer obligations for Nvidia to pay.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"group\": \"stated-over\",\n  \"source\": [\n   \"poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/stage-a\",\n   1\n  ],\n  \"constructed\": \"article + \\\"NVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\\\"; code-set due day and basis kept\",\n  \"first_readers\": null,\n  \"item\": {\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share exposure will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new AI-factory business model, and what contingent liability does that represent?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support/guarantees and revenue-share commitments NVIDIA discloses, and whether the exposure is material to its cash and credit risk.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K) after the July 2026 announcement\",\n    \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n    \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K), including its guarantees/contingent-liability and revenue-share disclosures\"\n   },\n   \"article\": {\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\",\n    \"text\": \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\\nNVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\"\n   },\n   \"fiscal_year_note\": \"NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-01 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\",\n   \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n    \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n    \"kind\": \"asset\",\n    \"focus\": \"The amount of credit support/guarantees and revenue-share commitments NVIDIA discloses, and whether the exposure is material to its cash and credit risk.\",\n    \"question\": \"What total USD amount of NVIDIA's credit support, guarantees, and revenue-share commitments under the AI-cloud business model does NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K for the year ended 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n    \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\",\n    \"rungs\": [\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"below 500,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 500,000,000 USD to below 2,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 2,000,000,000 USD to below 5,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 10,000,000,000 USD\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n      \"condition\": \"10,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"other_fields\": {\n    \"not_judged\": [\n     {\n      \"part\": \"Whether the exposure is material to NVIDIA's cash and credit risk, and what contingent liability it represents\",\n      \"why\": \"The tracked outcome names the amount disclosed; materiality and its interpretation are not part of that figure.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"reference\": null,\n    \"favourable_end\": \"lower\",\n    \"favourable_why\": \"Lower exposure means fewer contingent obligations and less potential downside to NVIDIA's cash and credit risk.\",\n    \"ruled_out_by\": null,\n    \"due_day_basis\": \"expected_publication\"\n   }\n  }\n }\n]\n\nFILE docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/expected.json\n{\n \"written\": \"2026-11-16, by Claude, from each candidate against the reader brief's D row, D414 and D210, before any reader of the diagnostic (round 106 sample: 24 distinct topics)\",\n \"expected\": {\n  \"1\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"2\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"3\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"4\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"5\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"6\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"7\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"8\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"9\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"10\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"11\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"12\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"An obligation already taken on, tracked by its FY2027 amount in the next annual report; neither the topic nor the article states the report's day (a topic's '~February' or 'early 2027' is an estimated month, not a stated day), so D414 sets 2027-04-01.\"\n  },\n  \"13\": {\n   \"D\": \"fails\",\n   \"why\": \"A spot price on one day (2026-12-31), not a whole year's figure, so D414's day does not apply; the topic dates its check at year-end 2026.\"\n  },\n  \"14\": {\n   \"D\": \"fails\",\n   \"why\": \"The figure covers the 12 months from 2026-07-07 (to mid-2027); the code-set 2027-03-31 falls before that period ends.\"\n  },\n  \"15\": {\n   \"D\": \"fails\",\n   \"why\": \"One quarter's change (Q4 2027 against Q4 2026), not a whole year's figure, so D414's 90-day day does not hold for it.\"\n  },\n  \"16\": {\n   \"D\": \"not a control\",\n   \"why\": \"A rolling twelve-month figure given its window's last day; the brief gives no rule for that day (round-105 checker); read and reported, not counted.\"\n  },\n  \"17\": {\n   \"D\": \"fails\",\n   \"why\": \"An agreed acquisition is an obligation already taken on, due by the brief at its next annual filing; the shown 2028-03-05 is an 18-month check day for an unsigned obligation.\"\n  },\n  \"18\": {\n   \"D\": \"fails\",\n   \"why\": \"As item 17: an agreed acquisition is due at the next annual filing, not at the 2028-03-24 check day.\"\n  },\n  \"19\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"A product event due on the last day of the year the topic names (2028-12-31).\"\n  },\n  \"20\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"A proposed, unsigned residual-value guarantee, due on the check day the topic states (2028-04-03).\"\n  },\n  \"21\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"A product event due on the day the topic states (2026-12-31).\"\n  },\n  \"22\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"A product event due on the day the topic states (2027-12-31).\"\n  },\n  \"23\": {\n   \"D\": \"holds\",\n   \"why\": \"CONSTRUCTED: the article states the annual report's filing day (2027-02-24) and the choices show that day with basis stated: a day the article states is kept.\"\n  },\n  \"24\": {\n   \"D\": \"fails\",\n   \"why\": \"CONSTRUCTED: the article states the annual report's filing day (2027-02-24), but the choices show code's 2027-04-01 with basis expected_publication: the day contradicts a date the article gives.\"\n  }\n },\n \"sentence_under_test\": \"An obligation's next annual filing is its annual report. Where the question asks for that year's figure and neither the thesis nor the article states the report's day, the due day is the one code sets for the year's figure (60 days after the fiscal year-end, D414), even where the fiscal-year note names an earlier month.\",\n \"adopt_rule\": \"Under the brief with the sentence: Codex holds D on at least 10 of items 1-12; both families fail D on at least 4 of items 13, 14, 15, 17, 18 each; both hold D on at least 3 of items 19-22 each; both hold D on item 23 and fail it on item 24. Not adopted if, in either family, a control among items 13-15, 17, 18, 19-22, 23 or 24 moves away from its expected mark under the sentence while it had that mark under the unchanged brief. Item 16 is read and reported, not counted.\"\n}\nBRIEF MATCHES\n17   is meant to hold on the due day). An event's rungs are `Yes`, then `Ruled out` when the step named something that can rule\n21   abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole after that day, stands on <due day>, and only when rung 1 does not\"), so that a\n42 - An event has at most three outcomes: it has happened by the due day; it is ruled out by the due day though it has not happened\n45   happened by the due day is `Yes` even if it is undone afterwards, unless the question asks whether it still stands. What\n46   rules an event out is a decision by someone (the company, an authority, a court, a party): the due day arriving without the\n50   that decision or conclusion is `Ruled out`; the proceeding still going on the due day is the last rung (D420). A\n68 - The due day is when the question can be resolved: a figure's publication date; for an event, the date its outcome is assessed. Where\n73   rolling twelve months), which is then a fault of the question's period and of the due day both.\n97 | `R` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | exactly one rung holds for every outcome. For a figure: the ranges meet end to end. For an event, where code writes the last rung: the `Yes` condition is the event the question asks about; the first sentence of the `Ruled out` condition, when there is one, is a decision against that event as a whole, or its abandonment or withdrawal as a whole, taken after the article's day and standing on the due day, and not the event's merely being still awaited, the refusal of one part of it, or a bar already in place when the article appeared (it need not list every way: code's sentence takes the others); and where the event can plainly be ruled out before the due day (a formal refusal, a block, an announced abandonment; for an authority's proceeding, its formal abandonment or withdrawal without the asked-for decision or conclusion) and the answer has no `Ruled out` rung, the mark fails; it fails too when `ruled_out_by` names no one who decides, or when the decision named is the day arriving, the event not having happened, or a decision to go on with a proceeding. Every condition is concrete and can be checked on the due day: none rests on a target or a threshold that is not yet set |\n98 | `D` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the due day follows the rule for its kind. A figure: the day the thesis or the article states, or, where neither states one, the day code sets from the year the figure covers (60 days after the company's fiscal year-end; 90 days after the calendar year-end for a theme's figure), which holds when the figure asked for is that year's figure. An event: the date the thesis or the article gives for assessing it, or the last day of the period it names, or the company's fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision with no date. It fails when the day contradicts a date the thesis or the article gives, falls before the figure's period ends, or, for an event, is a date none of these gives And `due_day_basis` says truly where the day comes from: `stated` only when the thesis or the article gives that day; `period_end` only when it is the last day of the period the thesis names, or the fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; `expected_publication` when the step set the day it expects the figure to be published. For a thesis whose `opens_as` is binding_obligation: an obligation already taken on is due at its next annual filing, and one not yet signed on the day by which the signing can be checked; another day fails |\nDECISION D414\n## D414 · 2026-11-10 · Code sets the due day of a year's figure whose day is an estimate\n\n- **Asked:** \"同一道“年报里的数字”题，模型三次估的到期日常差几天（比如 2 月 24 日和 2 月 28 日），34 道题里只有 18 道三次一致，你定的线是九成。年度数字题的到期日由谁定？\"\n  - Options: code sets it (recommended): \"公司财年结束后 60 天（美国大公司年报的法定期限）、行业日历年结束后 90 天（约定值）。回放结果 34 道里 32 道一致。代价：公司年报类的题比模型估的日子晚约一个月结案；行业数据偶尔可能比 90 天更晚；不是整年的数字（滚动 12 个月的调查、季度数）不能套这条规则，要先单独处理。\"; as now (the test still fails under line 6 and the work stays at this step).\n  - Alex: \"由程序统一定 (Recommended)\".\n- **Decided:** for a figure that covers one year and whose due day the model would estimate, code sets the day: the company's\n  fiscal year-end plus 60 days for a company's figure, the calendar year-end plus 90 days for a theme's figure. A day the topic\n  or the item states is kept. A figure that is not a year's (a survey's rolling twelve months, a quarter) is not given a day by\n  this rule and is dealt with first, on its own. The costs he was shown: a company's annual figure closes about a month after\n  the day the model expects it; an industry figure may come later than 90 days.\n- **Evidence behind the question:** `clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_a/result-2/due-day-rule-60-90.txt`.\n- **Scope:** the clean path's ladder step.\n\nDECISION D210\n## D210 · 2026-10-28 · A binding obligation already taken on opens as an amount at the next annual filing (role-v21, `poc-v37`)\n\n- Alex, after the arm (\"go 但是你不要只是看什么acp好不？你需要解决的是抽取质量问题 … 有用的，你得一条一条看是不是真的值得抽才知道你这些改动的抽取效果\").\n  Mechanism (two stored accounts, `docs/research/2026-10-28-extraction-quality/results/ask_admission.json`): D184/D185's sentence\n  \"due when its signing can be checked, at most 18 months out\" is written for a deal not yet signed; for a programme already announced\n  and signed (the AI Compute Partnership) the model proposes nothing or dates the question on the item day, which the code refuses.\n  Over the acceptance week the twelve ACP articles produced one proposal per run and none survived in `poc-v36`; 96 statements had no home.\n- Decided: role-v21 replaces that one sentence. Not yet signed: a yes/no question due after the item within 18 months (D185 unchanged).\n  Already taken on, one deal or a programme: a financing_amount question on the amount the obligation reaches at the company's next\n  annual filing, settled by its commitments note; the announcement is evidence to it. Entries and code checks as v20; `poc-v37` is the\n  replay default. Role 10,089 → 10,434 characters; the growth is the second form, which no other sentence covered.\n- Measured before the decision (`admission_arm.py`, production bytes as control, frozen reference, three draws): on the six ACP articles\n  whose text carries the obligation, control opened 0/18, the arm proposed 17/18 and the code kept 13/18 (two refused by the ladder-label\n  rule, D186); the four articles that report only the revenue share open nothing under both, rightly; the Reuters Ohio signing event\n  still opens (2/3, one refusal at the 548-day boundary). Acceptance: the same week replayed as `english-v37-nvda-1w-2026-10-28`, then\n  every dropped statement of the week read one by one against the analyst's standard (results.md).\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,re; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind\\\"); s=(p/\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md\\\").read_text().splitlines(); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(s[67:74])); a=json.loads((p/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\").read_text()); exec(\\\"for i,c in enumerate(a,1):\\\\n x=c[\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\"]; l=x[\\\\\\\"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\\\\\\\"]; print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nITEM\\\\\\\",i,\\\\\\\"CONSTRUCTED\\\\\\\",c.get(\\\\\\\"constructed\\\\\\\")); print(\\\\\\\"THESIS\\\\\\\",json.dumps(x[\\\\\\\"thesis\\\\\\\"],ensure_ascii=False)); print(\\\\\\\"FISCAL\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"fiscal_year_note\\\\\\\"]); print(\\\\\\\"LADDER\\\\\\\",json.dumps({k:l.get(k) for k in [\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"year\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"due_day\\\\\\\"]}),\\\\\\\"BASIS\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"other_fields\\\\\\\"].get(\\\\\\\"due_day_basis\\\\\\\")); print(\\\\\\\"ARTICLE\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"publication_date\\\\\\\"],x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\"]); print(\\\\\\\"EXCERPTS\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\".join(z.strip()[:500] for z in re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\"]) if re.search(r\\\\\\\"commit|obligation|guarantee|acqui|2027|2028|2026|twelve|12.month|quarter|annual|filing|file|deadline|launch\\\\\\\",z,re.I))[:2200])\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,re; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind\\\"); s=(p/\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md\\\").read_text().splitlines(); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(s[67:74])); a=json.loads((p/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\").read_text()); exec(\\\"for i,c in enumerate(a,1):\\\\n x=c[\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\"]; l=x[\\\\\\\"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\\\\\\\"]; print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nITEM\\\\\\\",i,\\\\\\\"CONSTRUCTED\\\\\\\",c.get(\\\\\\\"constructed\\\\\\\")); print(\\\\\\\"THESIS\\\\\\\",json.dumps(x[\\\\\\\"thesis\\\\\\\"],ensure_ascii=False)); print(\\\\\\\"FISCAL\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"fiscal_year_note\\\\\\\"]); print(\\\\\\\"LADDER\\\\\\\",json.dumps({k:l.get(k) for k in [\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"year\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"due_day\\\\\\\"]}),\\\\\\\"BASIS\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"other_fields\\\\\\\"].get(\\\\\\\"due_day_basis\\\\\\\")); print(\\\\\\\"ARTICLE\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"publication_date\\\\\\\"],x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\"]); print(\\\\\\\"EXCERPTS\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\".join(z.strip()[:500] for z in re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\"]) if re.search(r\\\\\\\"commit|obligation|guarantee|acqui|2027|2028|2026|twelve|12.month|quarter|annual|filing|file|deadline|launch\\\\\\\",z,re.I))[:2200])\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"- The due day is when the question can be resolved: a figure's publication date; for an event, the date its outcome is assessed. Where\n  the thesis or the article states the day a figure is published, that day is right. Where neither does (`due_day_basis` is\n  `expected_publication`), code sets the day and the step's own estimate is not shown: 60 days after the company's fiscal\n  year-end for a company's figure, 90 days after the calendar year-end for a theme's (D414). That day is right when the figure\n  is a figure for that year; it is wrong when the figure the question asks for is not a year's figure (a quarter, a survey's\n  rolling twelve months), which is then a fault of the question's period and of the due day both.\n- A company's figure for a quarter or a half-year is never a judged question: the step must decline it. A ladder written for\n\nITEM 1 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support business model?\", \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of credit support / revenue-share-backed capacity NVIDIA has committed to AI cloud operators under the announced model, as disclosed in its filings.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the model is presented as already being introduced/underway.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K) and interim 10-Qs/8-Ks disclosing guarantees, credit support and revenue-share commitments to AI cloud partners.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-01 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What total amount in USD of credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity has NVIDIA committed to AI cloud operators under its announced model, as disclosed in its FY2027 annual report?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-01 NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\nEXCERPTS Emerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\n\nITEM 2 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K (fiscal 2027), specifically its purchase obligations / contractual commitments disclosure and related notes.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-22 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What amount of purchase obligations and supply commitments will NVIDIA report as of FY2027 year-end in its FY2027 Form 10-K, according to its purchase-obligations and contractual-commitments disclosures and related notes?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-09-22 Top Analyst Reports for NVIDIA, Berkshire Hathaway & GE Aerospace\nEXCERPTS Tuesday, September 22, 2026 | China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees. | Even so, platform leadership, product cadence and expanding AI infrastructure demand give the positive drivers greater weight in the current risk-reward profile. | Recent acquisitions and renewed share repurchases show multiple avenues for deploying capital. | These offsets support an overall balanced risk-reward profile and a Neutral view. | The company raised its dividend by 30.6% to 36 cents per share in February 2026. | Per the Zacks analyst, solid IB business, strategic alliances/acquisitions, global footprint and Morgan Stanley's focus on less capital-markets-dependent operations will likely support its financials. | Per the Zacks analyst, strong Xifaxan growth, strategic acquisitions and new product approvals fuel Bausch Health's growth prospects.\n\nITEM 3 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments be at its next annual report, and could that obligation pressure its cash or credit position if AI demand disappoints?\", \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, against the ~$119 billion the item cites as the current reference.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the supply commitments are presented as already undertaken/outstanding.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K purchase commitments / contractual obligations disclosure and interim Form 10-Q updates.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What total dollar amount of NVIDIA supply purchase commitments will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as measured in the report\\u2019s purchase commitments / contractual obligations disclosure?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-06 How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nEXCERPTS NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in. | NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. | Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. | Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. | The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. | If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\n\nITEM 4 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How large is NVIDIA's equity investment in Intel, and will the $5 billion stake be completed and held at that level as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel and whether the reported $5 billion investment is completed, as disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report and any related 8-K / Schedule 13D-G filings.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the item presents the $5 billion as an equity investment/commitment allocated to Intel, i.e. a sized stake tracked by its amount at that report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, together with any 8-K or Schedule 13D/13G filings disclosing the size and closing of the Intel equity stake.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-01 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel will NVIDIA disclose as of FY2027 year-end (January 31, 2027), as stated in its FY2027 Form 10-K and any related 8-K or Schedule 13D/13G filings?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-01 Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nEXCERPTS Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. | This rally translates into a ~465% gain for the company's stock over the prior 12 months, climbing from a $18.97 low, a climb so massive that the 12-month average analyst price target of $93.93 now sits 33% behind the actual price of shares, creating an enduring wedge between where analysts have modelled the stock to end and where they find it today. | Q1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). | Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). | CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. | About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago. | Google's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis. | That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. | Add to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing. | Next earnings: July 23, 2026 | What Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance? | In Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY). | \"AI-linked revenue is now approximately 60% of the quarterly mix, as compared with a year ago when it was under 25%,\" said CFO David Zinsner. | The quarter was defined by unprecedented demand for silicon and Intel's determination t\n\nITEM 5 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much will Nvidia's maximum potential guarantee liability under the SB Energy/OpenAI affiliate infrastructure leases be at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The maximum potential amount guaranteed, any amounts paid or accrued, and the remaining term of the guarantees disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027), as fixed by the reason for an obligation already undertaken.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (Form 10-K), including the guarantees note and any disclosure of payments or accruals.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What maximum potential guarantee liability does Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 annual report (Form 10-K)?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-10-06 Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Which AI Chip Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\nEXCERPTS My preference is Nvidia, provided its software advantage continues to protect margins and its infrastructure guarantees do not absorb that benefit. | Nvidia's August 26 report showed revenue of $96.2 billion for the quarter ended July 26, up 106% year over year. | Historical ownership gives Nvidia broader long-side participation: Insider Monkey's hedge fund database recorded 285 holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1. | Broadcom's September 2 results reported $29.6 billion of quarterly revenue, up 86%, for the period ended August 2. | Its latest quarter also generated $13.7 billion of free cash flow, equal to 46% of revenue. | Infrastructure software supplied $8.75 billion of quarterly revenue. | Broadcom's five largest end customers accounted for 55% of quarterly revenue. | For scale, that contingent maximum equals nearly three-quarters of its $39.4 billion annual trailing free cash flow, although the potential obligations extend across several years. | Broadcom's hedge-fund-holder count fell to 170 in Q2 2026 from 173 in Q1. | It does not deduct every acquisition or strategic investment. | Nvidia's latest quarterly filing also disclosed guarantees signed in August, capped at $105 billion, supporting an OpenAI affiliate's infrastructure with SB Energy. | Payments depend on specified tenant defaults; guaranteed amounts decline over 20-year leases and cover defined portions of lease and power payments. | Broadcom's five-year rack backstop and Nvidia's longer infrastructure guarantees have different assets, remedies and timing; their headline maximums cannot establish which stock has the safer financing profile. | Supply obligations add another demand risk. | Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years, some adjustable or cancelable at a cost. | Broadcom disclosed $126.8 billion of unconditional purchase commitments, mainly inventory. | Neither total is an immediate cash bill, but slower customer spending could leave commitments harder to absorb. | Over two years, that requires growth of about 22.5% annually before providing capital appreciation. | That judgment would weaken if custom chips took enough economically at\n\nITEM 6 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, likely filed early 2027); set by the reason — an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's annual report on Form 10-K, purchase commitments / commitments and contingencies note, for the fiscal year ending late January 2027.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What outstanding amount of supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers does NVIDIA report as of the end of FY2027 in its Form 10-K purchase commitments / commitments and contingencies note?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-06 How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nEXCERPTS NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in. | NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. | Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. | Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. | The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. | If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\n\nITEM 7 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027); the item gives no date, so the date is set by the reason — an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K, in its contractual obligations / purchase commitments disclosure.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What will NVIDIA\\u2019s supply purchase commitments total in FY2027, as disclosed in its next Form 10-K contractual obligations or purchase commitments disclosure?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-06 How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nEXCERPTS NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in. | NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. | Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. | Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. | The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. | If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\n\nITEM 8 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much of Nvidia's $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments will remain outstanding or unconditional at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The total supply and capacity purchase commitments and the portion that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027), as fixed by the reason for an obligation already undertaken.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (Form 10-K), including the purchase obligations or commitments table.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What total amount of Nvidia\\u2019s supply and capacity purchase commitments will Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K, as of FY2027 year-end?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-10-06 Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Which AI Chip Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\nEXCERPTS My preference is Nvidia, provided its software advantage continues to protect margins and its infrastructure guarantees do not absorb that benefit. | Nvidia's August 26 report showed revenue of $96.2 billion for the quarter ended July 26, up 106% year over year. | Historical ownership gives Nvidia broader long-side participation: Insider Monkey's hedge fund database recorded 285 holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1. | Broadcom's September 2 results reported $29.6 billion of quarterly revenue, up 86%, for the period ended August 2. | Its latest quarter also generated $13.7 billion of free cash flow, equal to 46% of revenue. | Infrastructure software supplied $8.75 billion of quarterly revenue. | Broadcom's five largest end customers accounted for 55% of quarterly revenue. | For scale, that contingent maximum equals nearly three-quarters of its $39.4 billion annual trailing free cash flow, although the potential obligations extend across several years. | Broadcom's hedge-fund-holder count fell to 170 in Q2 2026 from 173 in Q1. | It does not deduct every acquisition or strategic investment. | Nvidia's latest quarterly filing also disclosed guarantees signed in August, capped at $105 billion, supporting an OpenAI affiliate's infrastructure with SB Energy. | Payments depend on specified tenant defaults; guaranteed amounts decline over 20-year leases and cover defined portions of lease and power payments. | Broadcom's five-year rack backstop and Nvidia's longer infrastructure guarantees have different assets, remedies and timing; their headline maximums cannot establish which stock has the safer financing profile. | Supply obligations add another demand risk. | Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years, some adjustable or cancelable at a cost. | Broadcom disclosed $126.8 billion of unconditional purchase commitments, mainly inventory. | Neither total is an immediate cash bill, but slower customer spending could leave commitments harder to absorb. | Over two years, that requires growth of about 22.5% annually before providing capital appreciation. | That judgment would weaken if custom chips took enough economically at\n\nITEM 9 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"What will NVIDIA's outstanding supply (purchase) commitments to its manufacturing and memory suppliers total at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The supply-commitments balance NVIDIA discloses in its next Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion reported in this item, and whether the obligation is growing faster than the revenue it supports (which would raise write-down or utilisation risk if hyperscaler orders slow).\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K, fiscal year ending late January 2027) — the next annual report for an obligation already undertaken, as section 3 fixes for such obligations.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K (fiscal 2027), which discloses purchase/supply commitments outstanding, with the 10-Q filings in between as interim checks.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What will NVIDIA's outstanding supply (purchase) commitments to its manufacturing and memory suppliers be as of FY2027 year-end, as disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-06 Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nEXCERPTS Blackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized. | Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling. | Watch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. | A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise.\n\nITEM 10 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027 (guarantees, commitments and contingencies note) and any 8-K or earnings disclosure quantifying the credit support for the additional AI lab's ~2 GW of computing capacity and the minimum-revenue guarantees to cloud partners.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-22 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-09-22 Is NVIDIA Funding Its Own Stock Rally?\nEXCERPTS If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. | That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. | NVIDIA’s revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027, so this number is easy to miss. | Management said in the fiscal Q2 2027 call that some will call NVIDIA’s support circular financing. | With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity. | Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters. | Management points to the size of its customers’ commitments. | OpenAI’s existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing. | OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030. | Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back. | NVIDIA’s next update comes at its fiscal Q3 2027 earnings call on November 17.\n\nITEM 11 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K), expected early 2027 — set by the reason: an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 Form 10-K (and the interim 10-Q purchase-commitments note), which disclose the dollar amount of supply/purchase commitments.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-06 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-06 How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nEXCERPTS NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in. | NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. | Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. | Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. | Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. | The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. | If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\n\nITEM 12 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much will Nvidia have guaranteed or committed in credit support for AI infrastructure projects at its next annual report, after agreeing in August 2026 to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported amount of Nvidia's guarantees / credit support for AI infrastructure projects, including the Ohio data center project, disclosed at its next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report: Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027 (filed in early 2027); set by the rule that an obligation already undertaken is tracked at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, including the guarantees and commitments note.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What amount of Nvidia's guarantees and credit support for AI infrastructure projects, including the Ohio project, will be disclosed in the guarantees and commitments note of its FY2027 Form 10-K?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-09-05 Jensen Huang Just Sent a Signal That Could Matter More Than the September Effect\nEXCERPTS He said Nvidia expects revenue to grow about 70% in its fiscal 2028, which begins Jan 31, 2027. | In its fiscal 2027 second quarter (which ended July 26), Nvidia's revenue soared by 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, including $89 billion in sales from the data center segment. | Wall Street analysts had previous expected the company to deliver fiscal 2028 revenue of about $570 billion, translating to growth of roughly 44%. | Supply constraints could limit Nvidia's fiscal 2028 growth | Nvidia exited the fiscal second quarter with $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up sequentially from $119 billion. | These commitments are primarily related to memory purchases and manufacturing capacity. | Of that total, $92 billion is scheduled for the rest of fiscal 2027, $87 billion for fiscal 2028, and $88 billion for fiscal 2029. | Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) Web Services plans to deploy another 2 million Nvidia GPUs in calendar years 2027 and 2028. | Nvidia also expects the combined capital expenditures of the top five hyperscalers to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027. | In such a scenario, Nvidia's large commitments to its own suppliers could result in higher costs. | In August, the company agreed to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio. | Huang also said AI labs for which Nvidia expects to use its balance sheet could account for roughly one-quarter of the company's business in fiscal 2028. | Nvidia expects its gross margins to fall from 75% in the fiscal second quarter to around 71% to 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter, before improving to around 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028. | By Manali Pradhan, CFA – Sep 5, 2026 at 4:54AM EST | Nvidia has increased its supply and capacity commitments from $119 billion to $279 billion as it prepares for future AI infrastructure demand.\n\nITEM 13 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will the spot rental price of an NVIDIA H100 GPU-hour keep falling through calendar 2026, dropping below the ~$3.20/hour peak reached in May 2026, as custom accelerators and easing GPU supply pressure the market price of compute?\", \"outcome\": \"The trajectory of H100 GPU-hour spot rental prices (Ornn compute-marketplace index) over calendar 2026 — whether the rate continues to decline from the ~$3.20/hour May 2026 peak.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 — checked against Ornn's published H100 spot rental-price index at year-end 2026.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Ornn's published spot-market H100 GPU-hour rental-price index (and comparable compute-marketplace price data) at year-end 2026.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-09 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What is Ornn's published H100 GPU-hour spot rental price, in USD per hour, measured on December 31, 2026?\", \"year\": \"2026\", \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-09 Nvidia is a victim of the compute marketplace it created\nEXCERPTS Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\n\nITEM 14 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, and how far will Nvidia's share of the Chinese accelerator market fall from its present level?\", \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budgets allocated to domestic products versus the ~30% baseline reported in the July 2026 Bloomberg Intelligence survey, and the direction of Nvidia's share of that market, as reported in follow-up surveys and Nvidia's disclosed China revenue.\", \"period\": \"The 12 months following the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (through roughly mid-2027); set by the survey's stated 'next 12 months' horizon, checked against follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosures over that window.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys of Chinese AI buyers, Chinese customs/import statistics for AI accelerators, domestic vendors' (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon) reported revenue, and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosure in its 10-Q filings.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-08 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What percentage of Chinese enterprises\\u2019 AI accelerator budgets will be allocated to domestic products over the 12 months following the Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on 2026-07-07, as established by follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys?\", \"year\": \"2026\", \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-08 Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators for domestic AI suppliers\nEXCERPTS Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\n\nITEM 15 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will DRAM/HBM contract prices remain elevated through calendar 2027, extending the shortage that has raised GPU makers' memory input costs?\", \"outcome\": \"Direction and level of DRAM/HBM contract prices through calendar 2027, specifically whether they remain above 2026 levels or normalize as Micron's 2027/2028 outlook suggests.\", \"period\": \"Full-year calendar 2027; checked against Q4 2027 DRAM contract price releases (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange) and memory makers' Q4 2027 results, reported roughly January 2028.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"TrendForce/DRAMeXchange quarterly and Q4 2027 DRAM contract price releases, plus Micron, Samsung and SK Hynix Q4 2027 results and supply commentary.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-12 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What will the year-over-year percentage change in DRAM/HBM contract prices be in Q4 2027 versus Q4 2026, as shown by TrendForce/DRAMeXchange\\u2019s Q4 2027 releases and memory makers\\u2019 Q4 2027 results?\", \"year\": \"2027\", \"due_day\": \"2028-03-30\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-12 Nvidia Skips New Gaming GPUs, Breaks 30-Year Streak [2026] - https://tech-insider.org/\nEXCERPTS Multiple reports confirm that, with September 2026 underway, the company has shipped zero new gaming GPUs all year and has nothing left on the calendar to change that — breaking a release streak that stretches back to the GeForce 256 ‘s debut in October 1999 . | According to The Information, Nvidia quietly shelved its planned RTX 50 “Super” refresh back in December 2025 — including a 24GB RTX 5080 Super that had reportedly reached completed-design stage — and reporting corroborated through September 2026 confirms the company’s next-generation RTX 60-series remains pushed back to 2028. | Nvidia Confirms No New Gaming GPUs Are Coming in 2026 | Timeline: How the 2026 Gaming GPU Gap Unfolded | RTX 60-Series and Rubin: Pushed to 2028 | What the Q1 2026 Numbers Show | The Software Workaround: DLSS, FSR, and Why They Matter More in 2026 | DLSS 5 Launches September 3 in NBA 2K27 | 2026 vs. | Could Nvidia Reverse Course Before 2028? | Will Nvidia Break Its Silence at CES 2027? | Signals Worth Watching Through the Rest of 2026 | Is Nvidia really skipping every new gaming GPU in 2026? | Why is a memory shortage stopping GPU launches? | What is DLSS 5 and when did it launch? | When will the RTX 60-series launch? | Could Nvidia unveil a surprise gaming GPU at CES 2027? | Should I still buy a gaming GPU in 2026? | Nvidia has shipped zero new gaming GPU architectures through September 2026 , breaking a release streak dating back roughly 30 years to the GeForce 256 in 1999. | The next-generation RTX 60-series , built on the Rubin platform, has slipped from a rumored late-2027 launch to 2028 . | The cause is a global memory shortage: Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have reportedly sold out their 2026 HBM output to AI data center customers. | The RTX 5060 has been pulled from retail, with RTX 50-series production cut an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026. | Nvidia’s most concrete gaming announcement of the year is software, not silicon: DLSS 5 launched September 3, 2026 , debuting in NBA 2K27 with day-one support on RTX 50-series GPUs and GeForce NOW . | The report first surfaced via The Information in early February 2026 and was independently corroborated by outlets inc\n\nITEM 16 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI-accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (e.g. Huawei Ascend and DeepSeek's in-house chips) over the next 12 months, and how much will that displace NVIDIA's China demand?\", \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese AI-accelerator budgets allocated to domestic suppliers, versus roughly 30% today, as reported in industry surveys/market research and reflected in NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue.\", \"period\": \"12 months from the item (by ~mid-2027), per the item's statement that Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Industry surveys/market-research estimates of Chinese AI-accelerator spending shares and NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue over the next 12 months (to mid-2027).\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-07 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What share of Chinese companies\\u2019 AI-accelerator budgets will go to domestic suppliers over the 12 months from 2026-07-07 to 2027-07-07, as estimated by industry surveys or market research?\", \"year\": \"2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-07-07\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-07 DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence\nEXCERPTS In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5] . | Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] .\n\nITEM 17 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will Nvidia complete its announced $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, the open-source AI model repository, by a check date within 18 months of the September 2026 announcement?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Nvidia closes (or abandons/restructures) the announced $12.9 billion purchase of Hugging Face, an acquisition that would absorb cash and add a developer-platform asset.\", \"period\": \"Check date within 18 months of the 2026-09-05 item (by approximately 2028-03-05); the item gives no closing date.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's Form 8-K and subsequent Form 10-Q/10-K reporting completion or termination of the Hugging Face acquisition, plus regulatory clearance announcements.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its announced acquisition of Hugging Face by March 5, 2028?\", \"year\": \"2028\", \"due_day\": \"2028-03-05\"} BASIS stated\nARTICLE 2026-09-05 John McGuire bought NVIDIA stock before NVIDIA made a $12.9 billion AI deal\nEXCERPTS This is a matter of public record; McGuire filed a disclosure form on Wednesday noting the purchase with the Office of the Clerk of the House of Representatives . | McGuire would know better than the rest of us just how valuable Hugging Face was on the open market because he serves on House Oversight subcommittees focused on cybersecurity, information technology and military affairs . | Assuming McGuire pays attention to what goes on in the subcommittee meetings, he’s getting access to classified and other sensitive information that the rest of us aren’t privy to, including the latest developments in who wants to buy who – valuable information, if you’re in a position to trade on it, like a congressman would be. | That information comes from a public disclosure form on file with the Office of the Clerk of the U.S. | House of Representatives – filed by McGuire, with his signature. | “I Don’t Even Do Trades” dumped more BlackRock stock – in separate trades valued between $1,001 and $15,000 – on July 10 and July 14 , per a filing made with the clerk’s office on Aug. | 7 filing also includes a notation of a sale of between $1,001 and $15,000 in stock of Applied Materials Inc ., a Silicon Valley company – market cap: $360.9 billion – that supplies equipment, services and software for the manufacture of semiconductor chips for electronics, flat panel displays for computers, smartphones, televisions, and solar products. | 7 filing: the sale of between $1,001 and $15,000 of stock in Palo Alto Networks Inc. | We have reached 57 percent of what we need to fund critical operations for 2026.\n\nITEM 18 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 8-K/press release announcing completion (or termination) of the Hugging Face acquisition, and the purchase-price and goodwill disclosures in its Form 10-Q/10-K.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-25 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\", \"year\": \"2028\", \"due_day\": \"2028-03-24\"} BASIS stated\nARTICLE 2026-09-25 Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\nEXCERPTS Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. | In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. | As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. | Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. | The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities. | In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. | As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. | This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions. | Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. | Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. | By Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST | Credo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026. | Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\n\nITEM 19 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system begin shipping to customers by the end of calendar 2028, following SemiAnalysis's report that its debut slipped from 2027 to 2028 (which NVIDIA disputes, saying its roadmap is intact)?\", \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\", \"period\": \"2028-12-31 (the item says the Kyber debut was pushed to 2028 from a 2027 plan; the check date is the end of that year. If NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact,' shipping in 2027 would also settle the by-end-2028 question affirmatively.)\", \"opens_as\": \"product_node\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA confirmation of first shipments of Vera Rubin Ultra/Kyber systems to cloud customers, or an explicit product-timeline disclosure in NVIDIA's annual report or quarterly earnings materials.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system have begun by December 31, 2028?\", \"year\": \"2028\", \"due_day\": \"2028-12-31\"} BASIS stated\nARTICLE 2026-07-05 Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\nEXCERPTS Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026. | Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. | The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 . | \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system. | The reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits. | SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\n\nITEM 20 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will Nvidia finalize its proposed sharing of up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values for its AI infrastructure financing platform by the check date?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\", \"period\": \"Check date 3 April 2028 (18 months after the 3 October 2026 item), with earlier disclosure possible in Nvidia's quarterly or annual filings.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's definitive agreement or SEC filing (10-Q/10-K) disclosing the finalized residual-value guarantee amount and terms for its AI infrastructure financing platform.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-10-03 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"Will Nvidia sign and disclose a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by 2028-04-03?\", \"year\": \"2028\", \"due_day\": \"2028-04-03\"} BASIS stated\nARTICLE 2026-10-03 Amazon Pursues $8 Billion Sale-and-Leaseback of Nvidia Chips\nEXCERPTS At the time, he stated: \"We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027.\" He added that \"the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary.\" The sale-leaseback structure is seen as a compromise to address the demand pressure he referenced while preserving financial soundness. | Nvidia also proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values. | However, the structure—in which Amazon guarantees long-term use as lessee—provides investors with a degree of cash flow stability.\n\nITEM 21 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin shipping by the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\", \"period\": \"2026-12-31 (the item says shipping could begin later in 2026, from a 2026-07-02 article)\", \"opens_as\": \"product_node\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's confirmation of first customer shipments of Vera Rubin, such as a press release, earnings call, or quarterly filing.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-02 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that it has made first customer shipments of its Vera Rubin AI chip platform by December 31, 2026?\", \"year\": \"2026\", \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\"} BASIS stated\nARTICLE 2026-07-02 Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?\nEXCERPTS CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027. | Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. | Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations. | The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. | Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years. | By Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\n\nITEM 22 CONSTRUCTED None\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\", \"period\": \"2027-12-31 check date — the item (published 2026-07-05) says Kyber was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 and has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, so end-2027 discriminates the original schedule from the reported delay.\", \"opens_as\": \"product_node\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's own shipment and roadmap disclosures — GTC keynote/product briefings, quarterly earnings commentary on Rubin Ultra revenue timing, and cloud-partner (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud) deployment confirmations — checked at year-end 2027.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"Will NVIDIA make a first shipment of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system paired with Vera Rubin Ultra by December 31, 2027, as confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement?\", \"year\": \"2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\"} BASIS stated\nARTICLE 2026-07-05 Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\nEXCERPTS Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026. | Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. | The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 . | \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system. | The reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits. | SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\n\nITEM 23 CONSTRUCTED article + \"NVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\"; due day 2027-02-24, basis stated\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much will Nvidia's commitments to suppliers (purchase obligations, primarily memory procurement) be at its next annual report, after rising to $279 billion in the second quarter from $119 billion the prior quarter?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported amount of Nvidia's commitments to suppliers / purchase obligations disclosed at its next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report: Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027 (filed in early 2027); set by the rule that an obligation already undertaken is tracked at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's next annual report on Form 10-K, including its disclosure of purchase commitments and contractual obligations to suppliers.\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-09-05 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What amount of Nvidia\\u2019s supplier commitments (purchase obligations, primarily memory procurement) will Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K, as of fiscal year-end?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-02-24\"} BASIS stated\nARTICLE 2026-09-05 Recent News From Nvidia and SK Hynix Reveals Exactly What the Market Expects for Micron Technology's Future\nEXCERPTS Nvidia just made a huge commitment to memory chips | Nvidia's second-quarter earnings report included a small detail that could have a huge impact on Micron and the rest of the memory chip industry. | The company increased its commitments to suppliers to $279 billion, up from $119 billion in the previous quarter. | All three leading chipmakers started signing strategic agreements to guarantee demand well into the future in exchange for locking in prices today. | That gives the companies the confidence to build new capacity with a guaranteed buyer at the end of the day. | The guaranteed revenue could reduce the cyclicality that has historically plagued memory chip stocks. | So, the fact that Nvidia made a huge commitment should be a positive signal for Micron stock. | More importantly, the analyst consensus has been that supply will catch up to demand by 2028 and revenue growth will slow for the memory chipmakers. | Micron's most recent guidance was that tight conditions will \"persist beyond calendar 2027.\" | By Adam Levy – Sep 5, 2026 at 7:45AM EST | NVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\n\nITEM 24 CONSTRUCTED article + \"NVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\"; code-set due day and basis kept\nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share exposure will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new AI-factory business model, and what contingent liability does that represent?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support/guarantees and revenue-share commitments NVIDIA discloses, and whether the exposure is material to its cash and credit risk.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K) after the July 2026 announcement\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K), including its guarantees/contingent-liability and revenue-share disclosures\"}\nFISCAL NVDA's fiscal year ends in month 1 and is named for the calendar year in which it ends: FY2027 runs from 2026-02 to 2027-01; the publication day 2026-07-01 falls in FY2027, so this year is FY2027 and next year is FY2028; FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due date.\nLADDER {\"question\": \"What total USD amount of NVIDIA's credit support, guarantees, and revenue-share commitments under the AI-cloud business model does NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K for the year ended 2027-01-31?\", \"year\": \"FY2027\", \"due_day\": \"2027-04-01\"} BASIS expected_publication\nARTICLE 2026-07-01 NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\nEXCERPTS Emerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute. | NVIDIA said it will file its fiscal 2027 annual report on Form 10-K on February 24, 2027.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\"); a=json.loads(p.read_text()); exec(\\\"for i in range(8,19):\\\\n x=a[i-1][\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\"]; l=x[\\\\\\\"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\\\\\\\"]; print(i,json.dumps(x[\\\\\\\"thesis\\\\\\\"],ensure_ascii=False),\\\\\\\"QUESTION\\\\\\\",l[\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"DUE\\\\\\\",l[\\\\\\\"due_day\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"BASIS\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"other_fields\\\\\\\"].get(\\\\\\\"due_day_basis\\\\\\\"))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\"); a=json.loads(p.read_text()); exec(\\\"for i in range(8,19):\\\\n x=a[i-1][\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\"]; l=x[\\\\\\\"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\\\\\\\"]; print(i,json.dumps(x[\\\\\\\"thesis\\\\\\\"],ensure_ascii=False),\\\\\\\"QUESTION\\\\\\\",l[\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"DUE\\\\\\\",l[\\\\\\\"due_day\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"BASIS\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"other_fields\\\\\\\"].get(\\\\\\\"due_day_basis\\\\\\\"))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"8 {\"description\": \"How much of Nvidia's $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments will remain outstanding or unconditional at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The total supply and capacity purchase commitments and the portion that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027), as fixed by the reason for an obligation already undertaken.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (Form 10-K), including the purchase obligations or commitments table.\"} QUESTION What total amount of Nvidia’s supply and capacity purchase commitments will Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K, as of FY2027 year-end? DUE 2027-04-01 BASIS expected_publication\n9 {\"description\": \"What will NVIDIA's outstanding supply (purchase) commitments to its manufacturing and memory suppliers total at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The supply-commitments balance NVIDIA discloses in its next Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion reported in this item, and whether the obligation is growing faster than the revenue it supports (which would raise write-down or utilisation risk if hyperscaler orders slow).\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K, fiscal year ending late January 2027) — the next annual report for an obligation already undertaken, as section 3 fixes for such obligations.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K (fiscal 2027), which discloses purchase/supply commitments outstanding, with the 10-Q filings in between as interim checks.\"} QUESTION What will NVIDIA's outstanding supply (purchase) commitments to its manufacturing and memory suppliers be as of FY2027 year-end, as disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K? DUE 2027-04-01 BASIS expected_publication\n10 {\"description\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027 (guarantees, commitments and contingencies note) and any 8-K or earnings disclosure quantifying the credit support for the additional AI lab's ~2 GW of computing capacity and the minimum-revenue guarantees to cloud partners.\"} QUESTION What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments? DUE 2027-04-01 BASIS expected_publication\n11 {\"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\", \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K), expected early 2027 — set by the reason: an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 Form 10-K (and the interim 10-Q purchase-commitments note), which disclose the dollar amount of supply/purchase commitments.\"} QUESTION What dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K? DUE 2027-04-01 BASIS expected_publication\n12 {\"description\": \"How much will Nvidia have guaranteed or committed in credit support for AI infrastructure projects at its next annual report, after agreeing in August 2026 to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported amount of Nvidia's guarantees / credit support for AI infrastructure projects, including the Ohio data center project, disclosed at its next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report: Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027 (filed in early 2027); set by the rule that an obligation already undertaken is tracked at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, including the guarantees and commitments note.\"} QUESTION What amount of Nvidia's guarantees and credit support for AI infrastructure projects, including the Ohio project, will be disclosed in the guarantees and commitments note of its FY2027 Form 10-K? DUE 2027-04-01 BASIS expected_publication\n13 {\"description\": \"Will the spot rental price of an NVIDIA H100 GPU-hour keep falling through calendar 2026, dropping below the ~$3.20/hour peak reached in May 2026, as custom accelerators and easing GPU supply pressure the market price of compute?\", \"outcome\": \"The trajectory of H100 GPU-hour spot rental prices (Ornn compute-marketplace index) over calendar 2026 — whether the rate continues to decline from the ~$3.20/hour May 2026 peak.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 — checked against Ornn's published H100 spot rental-price index at year-end 2026.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Ornn's published spot-market H100 GPU-hour rental-price index (and comparable compute-marketplace price data) at year-end 2026.\"} QUESTION What is Ornn's published H100 GPU-hour spot rental price, in USD per hour, measured on December 31, 2026? DUE 2027-03-31 BASIS expected_publication\n14 {\"description\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, and how far will Nvidia's share of the Chinese accelerator market fall from its present level?\", \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budgets allocated to domestic products versus the ~30% baseline reported in the July 2026 Bloomberg Intelligence survey, and the direction of Nvidia's share of that market, as reported in follow-up surveys and Nvidia's disclosed China revenue.\", \"period\": \"The 12 months following the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (through roughly mid-2027); set by the survey's stated 'next 12 months' horizon, checked against follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosures over that window.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys of Chinese AI buyers, Chinese customs/import statistics for AI accelerators, domestic vendors' (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon) reported revenue, and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosure in its 10-Q filings.\"} QUESTION What percentage of Chinese enterprises’ AI accelerator budgets will be allocated to domestic products over the 12 months following the Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on 2026-07-07, as established by follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys? DUE 2027-03-31 BASIS expected_publication\n15 {\"description\": \"Will DRAM/HBM contract prices remain elevated through calendar 2027, extending the shortage that has raised GPU makers' memory input costs?\", \"outcome\": \"Direction and level of DRAM/HBM contract prices through calendar 2027, specifically whether they remain above 2026 levels or normalize as Micron's 2027/2028 outlook suggests.\", \"period\": \"Full-year calendar 2027; checked against Q4 2027 DRAM contract price releases (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange) and memory makers' Q4 2027 results, reported roughly January 2028.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"TrendForce/DRAMeXchange quarterly and Q4 2027 DRAM contract price releases, plus Micron, Samsung and SK Hynix Q4 2027 results and supply commentary.\"} QUESTION What will the year-over-year percentage change in DRAM/HBM contract prices be in Q4 2027 versus Q4 2026, as shown by TrendForce/DRAMeXchange’s Q4 2027 releases and memory makers’ Q4 2027 results? DUE 2028-03-30 BASIS expected_publication\n16 {\"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI-accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (e.g. Huawei Ascend and DeepSeek's in-house chips) over the next 12 months, and how much will that displace NVIDIA's China demand?\", \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese AI-accelerator budgets allocated to domestic suppliers, versus roughly 30% today, as reported in industry surveys/market research and reflected in NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue.\", \"period\": \"12 months from the item (by ~mid-2027), per the item's statement that Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Industry surveys/market-research estimates of Chinese AI-accelerator spending shares and NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue over the next 12 months (to mid-2027).\"} QUESTION What share of Chinese companies’ AI-accelerator budgets will go to domestic suppliers over the 12 months from 2026-07-07 to 2027-07-07, as estimated by industry surveys or market research? DUE 2027-07-07 BASIS expected_publication\n17 {\"description\": \"Will Nvidia complete its announced $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, the open-source AI model repository, by a check date within 18 months of the September 2026 announcement?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Nvidia closes (or abandons/restructures) the announced $12.9 billion purchase of Hugging Face, an acquisition that would absorb cash and add a developer-platform asset.\", \"period\": \"Check date within 18 months of the 2026-09-05 item (by approximately 2028-03-05); the item gives no closing date.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's Form 8-K and subsequent Form 10-Q/10-K reporting completion or termination of the Hugging Face acquisition, plus regulatory clearance announcements.\"} QUESTION Will NVIDIA complete its announced acquisition of Hugging Face by March 5, 2028? DUE 2028-03-05 BASIS stated\n18 {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 8-K/press release announcing completion (or termination) of the Hugging Face acquisition, and the purchase-price and goodwill disclosures in its Form 10-Q/10-K.\"} QUESTION Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings? DUE 2028-03-24 BASIS stated\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,re; a=json.loads(pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\").read_text()); exec(\\\"for n in [1,4,10,17]:\\\\n x=a[n-1][\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\"]; print(\\\\\\\"ITEM\\\\\\\",n); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s[:1500] for s in re.split(r\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\"]) if re.search(r\\\\\\\"hugging|invest|credit|guarantee|revenue.shar|commit\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,re; a=json.loads(pathlib.Path(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/candidates.json\\\").read_text()); exec(\\\"for n in [1,4,10,17]:\\\\n x=a[n-1][\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\"]; print(\\\\\\\"ITEM\\\\\\\",n); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s[:1500] for s in re.split(r\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",x[\\\\\\\"article\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\"]) if re.search(r\\\\\\\"hugging|invest|credit|guarantee|revenue.shar|commit\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"ITEM 1\nNVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\nITEM 4\nIntel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. While currently trading above consensus analyst targets, the valuation is supported by evolving commercial fundamentals. Technically, the stock remains in a neutral-bullish trend, with a potential move toward $159.90 if it clears $150.40 resistance.\nIntel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago.\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis. Google inked a multiyear deal for continued use of Intel Xeon processors in Google's workload-optimized instances, including the newest Xeon 6 processors that power C4 and N4 instances. The companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing. That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\nNVIDIA selection: Xeon 6 chosen as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8; $5B equity investment\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\nNVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next product cycle? Here's the explanation from Lip-Bu Tan: \"The CPU has become the orchestration layer, the control plane of the entire AI infrastructure. In inference workloads, we're starting to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs towards 1 CPU to 1 GPU.\" Jensen Huang's choice of Xeon 6 is a product architecture decision, by the company that's the benchmark for defining AI infrastructure, not an investment, or a promotion, from NVIDIA: Jensen's selection of Xeon 6 is the best possible validation of Intel's CPU competitive position.\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY). Non-GAAP earnings per share was $0.29 per share, compared with $0.73 (GAAP) because of foundry capital investment charges for 18A. Intel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion. \"AI-linked revenue is now approximately 60% of the quarterly mix, as compared with a year ago when it was under 25%,\" said CFO David Zinsner. The quarter was defined by unprecedented demand for silicon and Intel's determination to use the entire factory network as a means to maximize output. \"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\" said CFO Zinsner.\nThat's a 33% difference, which shows how rapidly the Intel story has evolved relative to the time required for sell-side equity research analysts to update their models. Cantor Fitzgerald increased its 12-month price target to $150 on July 1 while keeping a Neutral rating, stating that the new price target was 13.9% above the current stock price. The majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A. The Simple Wall St analysis places \"fair value\" Price/Sales of 15.1 vs. 13.1, which suggests that Intel is not significantly overvalued vs. peer group P/S at current trading levels.\nIntel is up 465% over the past year and now trades 33% above the consensus analyst 12-month price target of $93.93. That gap exists because the story, now anchored around NVIDIA's investment and product choice, the Google and Microsoft signed deals, has moved a long distance relative to the ability of sell-side research analysts to update models. Those commercial agreements are no longer a rumour.\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\nITEM 10\nHere is how this kind of support works, as a made-up example. A chipmaker invests in a young customer. That customer then spends the money on the chipmaker’s products.\nManagement says a surge in AI demand is driving a global buildout of computing infrastructure. It also describes revenue-sharing deals with cloud partners.\nNVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, according to management. For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity. With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\nThose lab investments are part of a broader expansion: Business Insider reported that NVIDIA’s overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion. In summer 2025, that portfolio was roughly $7 billion. That is a 14-fold increase, so this is not a mild change.\nThere is also a change in how quickly NVIDIA gets paid. Days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days. Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters. So some buyers with strong credit are taking longer to pay.\nThe price likely assumes that growth holds up and that the backed customers keep paying . Management points to the size of its customers’ commitments. OpenAI’s existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing. OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030. Whether those purchases rest on NVIDIA’s backing is worth watching.\nRoughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back. That is a notable share, and it represents a key risk factor that investors should monitor. The worry would grow if that share climbs or collection times keep stretching. It would ease if more growth comes from customers paying their own way. NVIDIA’s next update comes at its fiscal Q3 2027 earnings call on November 17.\nITEM 17\nMAGA Congressman John McGuire , Mr. “I Don’t Even Trades,” bought between $1,001 and $15,000 of NVIDIA stock ahead of NVIDIA’s announcement this week that it is buying AI startup Hugging Face .\nOn Thursday, NVIDIA – market cap: $5.55 trillion – announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face, a New York-based startup that has built a repository for open-source AI models.\nHugging Face was the focus of an intense bidding war given its place in the AI sphere – it has more than 18 million developers and researchers using more than 3 million models, and more than 200,000 enterprise customers.\nMcGuire would know better than the rest of us just how valuable Hugging Face was on the open market because he serves on House Oversight subcommittees focused on cybersecurity, information technology and military affairs .\nAssuming McGuire pays attention to what goes on in the subcommittee meetings, he’s getting access to classified and other sensitive information that the rest of us aren’t privy to, including the latest developments in who wants to buy who – valuable information, if you’re in a position to trade on it, like a congressman would be.\nOf note here: McGuire, oddly, doesn’t seem to want to take credit for using the inside info to better his own personal bottom line.\nCredit here on the stock trades becoming public knowledge in the first place goes to Cville Right Now , which broke the story on McGuire actually doing trades , reporting back on July 13 that McGuire, after voting with the House majority to pass the 21st Century Road to Housing Act “sold off over $1,000 of BlackRock Inc. stock , partially divesting himself from one of the companies the legislation targets three days before the bill became law,” and “also divulged purchasing between $1,001 and $15,000 in Space Explorations Technologies Corp ., the company founded by Elon Musk in 2002.”\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"n\":1,\"expected_right\":false,\"why\":\"The article says NVIDIA is 'introducing a new business model,' but does not establish that a credit-support obligation has already been taken on; the FY2027 figure and code-set date nevertheless support the holds mark.\"}\n{\"n\":2,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"NVIDIA 'is taking on larger supply commitments,' and the FY2027 figure uses the code-set date because no filing day is stated.\"}\n{\"n\":3,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The article reports '$119B in supply commitments,' and the question asks for the amount in the next annual report without a stated filing day.\"}\n{\"n\":4,\"expected_right\":false,\"why\":\"The article says NVIDIA 'invested $5B into Intel,' which establishes an investment but does not establish an outstanding binding obligation; the FY2027 figure and code-set date nevertheless support the holds mark.\"}\n{\"n\":5,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The article reports 'guarantees signed in August,' and the FY2027 annual-report figure has no stated publication day.\"}\n{\"n\":6,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The item reports existing 'supply commitments' and asks for their FY2027 annual-report amount without a stated filing day.\"}\n{\"n\":7,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The item reports '$119B in supply commitments,' and the question asks for their FY2027 total in the next Form 10-K.\"}\n{\"n\":8,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The topic asks for existing supply and capacity commitments at the next annual report, with no stated filing day.\"}\n{\"n\":9,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The question asks for outstanding purchase commitments at FY2027 year-end in the next Form 10-K, with no stated filing day.\"}\n{\"n\":10,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The article says NVIDIA 'guarantees a minimum level of revenue' to some cloud partners, and the question asks for the FY2027 disclosed total without a stated publication day.\"}\n{\"n\":11,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The topic tracks existing supply commitments at the FY2027 Form 10-K, and 'early 2027' does not state a filing day.\"}\n{\"n\":12,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The topic says NVIDIA agreed to guarantees in August 2026 and asks for the amount in its next annual report without a stated filing day.\"}\n{\"n\":13,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The question asks for a spot price 'measured on December 31, 2026,' rather than a figure for the whole year.\"}\n{\"n\":14,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The shown 2027-03-31 due day falls before the '12 months following' 2026-07-07 end.\"}\n{\"n\":15,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The question asks for Q4 2027 versus Q4 2026, and the brief says the code-set annual date is wrong for 'a quarter.'\"}\n{\"n\":16,\"expected_right\":false,\"why\":\"The brief says the code-set date is wrong when the figure is 'a survey’s rolling twelve months,' and 2027-07-07 is not a stated publication day.\"}\n{\"n\":17,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The article reports an announced '$12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face,' while the shown date is an 18-month completion check rather than the next annual filing.\"}\n{\"n\":18,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The article says NVIDIA 'agreed to acquire Hugging Face,' so the brief calls for the next annual filing rather than the 18-month completion check.\"}\n{\"n\":19,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The topic states '2028-12-31' as the check date, and the choices use that date with basis 'stated.'\"}\n{\"n\":20,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The article says NVIDIA 'proposed' the guarantee, and the topic states 2028-04-03 as the signing check date.\"}\n{\"n\":21,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The topic states '2026-12-31' as the check date, and the choices use that date with basis 'stated.'\"}\n{\"n\":22,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The topic states '2027-12-31 check date,' and the choices use that date with basis 'stated.'\"}\n{\"n\":23,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The constructed article says NVIDIA will file the annual report on 'February 24, 2027,' and the choices use that day with basis 'stated.'\"}\n{\"n\":24,\"expected_right\":true,\"why\":\"The constructed article states a filing day of 'February 24, 2027,' but the choices use 2027-04-01 with basis 'expected_publication.'\"}"}}
